WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.980 --> 00:00:16.670
[ Applause ]
Diane Greene: Thank you so much, and welcome!

00:00:16.670 --> 00:00:21.519
This is just an incredibly exciting event
for all of us in Google Cloud, for all of

00:00:21.519 --> 00:00:23.699
us at Google, I would say.

00:00:23.699 --> 00:00:27.689
You know, the guy that was mic'ing me was
telling me that there was a queue around the

00:00:27.689 --> 00:00:34.050
block, that we had a blockbuster, and I'm
sorry for the queue, but, you know, we're

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:40.510
pretty happy to have all of you here to be
at capacity at 10,000, all the people live-streaming.

00:00:40.510 --> 00:00:47.170
I really regret the live-streamers couldn't
see the surround on that amazing animation

00:00:47.170 --> 00:00:51.460
that we just showed.

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:56.420
You know, Google -- I mean, cloud, cloud,
does anybody not agree it's the biggest thing

00:00:56.420 --> 00:00:57.859
going on in I.T.

00:00:57.859 --> 00:00:58.859
right now?

00:00:58.859 --> 00:01:01.829
[ Applause ]
Diane Greene: Yeah.

00:01:01.829 --> 00:01:02.829
Whoo!

00:01:02.829 --> 00:01:08.650
And Google Cloud -- I mean, the adoption of
cloud in general, I believe it must be accelerating.

00:01:08.650 --> 00:01:13.660
I know at Google Cloud this year we've seen
unbelievable acceleration.

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:17.100
What's that?

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:18.820
Okay.

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:20.540
Okay.

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:25.690
And the quality of our customer conversations
are really changing.

00:01:25.690 --> 00:01:32.670
We -- you know, for a while it was more "let's
use BigQuery.

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:34.030
Let's do our data analytics.

00:01:34.030 --> 00:01:36.180
Let's do our machine learning project."

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:41.130
But just two weeks ago, I met with five customers
over the course of the week, and three of

00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:43.420
them said, "Hey, I want to do a full lift
and shift.

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:46.490
I just want to move everything to the cloud."

00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:51.720
And it was only last September that we actually
started calling ourselves "Google Cloud."

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:59.610
All that we do from the infrastructure to
the workplace collaboration, productivity,

00:01:59.610 --> 00:02:04.450
and even the mobile devices in the enterprise.

00:02:04.450 --> 00:02:05.880
It's been a remarkable year.

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:11.740
I mean, our engineers have done over 500 releases.

00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:17.150
These are releases to get functionality to
our customers, these are new, innovations.

00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:27.959
These are inventions, and just ongoing improvement
in all that we do: Security, reliability,

00:02:27.959 --> 00:02:30.109
efficiencies.

00:02:30.109 --> 00:02:34.140
Cloud is just an incredibly cool place to
be working right now.

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:39.720
It's basically revolutionizing -- it's sort
of where a lot of the revolution, the digital

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:43.840
revolution for every industry is going on.

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:52.400
In financial services, you know, tremendous
efficiencies on how data is processed or how

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:56.360
the banks can talk directly to their customers.

00:02:56.360 --> 00:03:02.469
In health, we're seeing a revolution in diagnosis,
in predicting patient outcomes.

00:03:02.469 --> 00:03:08.549
In retail, particularly exciting area, taking
billions of dollars out as we revolutionize

00:03:08.549 --> 00:03:10.950
the store of the future.

00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:17.240
Media, something Google is particularly well
situated to do.

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:25.329
And energy, manufacturing, optimizing, reducing
the energy usage, making the factories -- making

00:03:25.329 --> 00:03:29.069
the factories, the manufacturing, more efficient.

00:03:29.069 --> 00:03:31.340
It's incredibly exciting to be part of this.

00:03:31.340 --> 00:03:33.140
We have such interesting customers.

00:03:33.140 --> 00:03:38.900
We have a customer, Planet Labs, that is taking
pictures of the entire Earth every three hours

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:43.159
and that's going on Google Cloud.

00:03:43.159 --> 00:03:51.329
I think the cloud, you know, it's no longer
sort of a utility for surplus peak capacity.

00:03:51.329 --> 00:03:54.629
It's no longer a place just to store things.

00:03:54.629 --> 00:03:59.319
You know, it's start-ups, it's where they
start but it's not solely for a start-up to

00:03:59.319 --> 00:04:06.439
figure out what demand they're going to have
before they spend any money.

00:04:06.439 --> 00:04:12.499
Workplace productivity has really moved beyond
sending files around via email.

00:04:12.499 --> 00:04:18.609
And the cloud is really what gives -- what's
giving -- increasingly giving customers their

00:04:18.609 --> 00:04:21.340
competitive advantage.

00:04:21.340 --> 00:04:23.330
And what's important there?

00:04:23.330 --> 00:04:27.040
Well, paramount is security.

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:33.390
From the board level on down, nobody -- you
know, everybody really cares about security.

00:04:33.390 --> 00:04:37.340
And I'll say a little more about security
at scale in a little bit.

00:04:37.340 --> 00:04:45.850
It's about cost, performance, effectivity,
the flexible costs, the lock-in, and it's

00:04:45.850 --> 00:04:51.570
about reliability, and I'm just -- because
of all the interest in reliability recently,

00:04:51.570 --> 00:04:55.220
I'm just going to say a few words.

00:04:55.220 --> 00:04:59.140
You know, you take Google Search.

00:04:59.140 --> 00:05:04.340
It was designed -- it runs -- as you see when
you do a search, it runs at five-ninths of

00:05:04.340 --> 00:05:05.760
availability.

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:10.040
That's 99.999% up-time.

00:05:10.040 --> 00:05:15.130
And we're -- our cloud, that's how we designed
our cloud and we're making it really easy

00:05:15.130 --> 00:05:21.290
for our customers to design and deploy for
that kind of reliability.

00:05:21.290 --> 00:05:24.290
Distributed, no single point of failure.

00:05:24.290 --> 00:05:25.290
And I'm really proud.

00:05:25.290 --> 00:05:32.250
I just learned yesterday that we were recognized
as being -- having the highest availability

00:05:32.250 --> 00:05:36.350
of any cloud over the course of 2016.

00:05:36.350 --> 00:05:43.520
[ Applause ]
Diane Greene: Thank you.

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:47.340
I think 2017 will be promising too, you know.

00:05:47.340 --> 00:05:50.910
[ Laughter ]
Diane Greene: We're putting a lot of effort

00:05:50.910 --> 00:05:55.830
across the board, across our entire systems,
with the unbelievably excellent engineers

00:05:55.830 --> 00:05:59.990
we have, to keep giving you more nines.

00:05:59.990 --> 00:06:00.990
What else?

00:06:00.990 --> 00:06:05.400
Well, the world, customers, are getting increasingly
focused on data.

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:09.780
You want the world's best data analytics,
the world's best machine learning.

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:12.860
This is definitely a Google strength.

00:06:12.860 --> 00:06:14.740
And then finally, workplace culture.

00:06:14.740 --> 00:06:18.480
You know, really it's about realtime collaboration.

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:23.340
It's about finding ways to get the bureaucracy
out of a company because everybody's got to

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:29.400
move fast now, and we have the technology
tools to do that.

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:33.940
So over the next three days, you're going
to hear from our engineers.

00:06:33.940 --> 00:06:35.570
They're going to tell you about our products.

00:06:35.570 --> 00:06:37.740
They're going to give you our roadmaps.

00:06:37.740 --> 00:06:40.930
You're going to see lots of demos.

00:06:40.930 --> 00:06:47.730
Our customer-facing people will help you navigate
Google Cloud.

00:06:47.730 --> 00:06:52.310
And you're also, in this keynote, going to
hear from our customers.

00:06:52.310 --> 00:06:57.070
Everybody at Google Cloud is so incredibly
proud of the customers that we have here today

00:06:57.070 --> 00:06:59.510
that we've been working with over the course
of the year.

00:06:59.510 --> 00:07:04.050
Six customers and a major partner.

00:07:04.050 --> 00:07:06.250
So now I just want to pause.

00:07:06.250 --> 00:07:07.940
I'm wearing my red ribbon.

00:07:07.940 --> 00:07:12.040
I want to acknowledge that it's International
Women's Day today.

00:07:12.040 --> 00:07:19.550
[ Cheers and applause ]
Diane Greene: Wow!

00:07:19.550 --> 00:07:28.380
I have to say that response is pretty exciting.

00:07:28.380 --> 00:07:35.720
You know, this industry, I think I've been
incredibly fortunate, but it was sort of an

00:07:35.720 --> 00:07:41.500
industry where I -- you know, I was lucky
and I kind of was -- chose to be kind of oblivious

00:07:41.500 --> 00:07:47.610
to what was going on, and now we're in an
environment where women are just increasingly

00:07:47.610 --> 00:07:55.570
having a huge impact and adding a lot of value
to our industry, and women are celebrated

00:07:55.570 --> 00:07:58.790
if they raise their hand and they say, "hey,
you're missing my value.

00:07:58.790 --> 00:08:01.770
You're not recognizing what I'm doing."

00:08:01.770 --> 00:08:07.690
And at Google, we strive at Google Cloud to
have an environment where no one needs to

00:08:07.690 --> 00:08:16.620
raise their hand, but no matter what, it's
completely safe to do that.

00:08:16.620 --> 00:08:24.570
You know, I really look forward to the day
when this audience is maybe 50% women.

00:08:24.570 --> 00:08:29.450
It's more fun to have diversity.

00:08:29.450 --> 00:08:37.229
So shifting gears, now I want to bring on
our next keynote speaker.

00:08:37.229 --> 00:08:44.259
It's my phenomenal privilege to introduce
our incredibly smart, compassionate, and just

00:08:44.259 --> 00:08:53.160
great leader, Sundar Pichai.

00:08:53.160 --> 00:09:01.620
[ Applause ]
Sundar Pichai: Thank you.

00:09:01.620 --> 00:09:03.069
This is a big crowd.

00:09:03.069 --> 00:09:09.339
I've done many I/O presentations here, and
it feels like a bigger crowd than Google I/O.

00:09:09.339 --> 00:09:11.600
So thank you all for joining today.

00:09:11.600 --> 00:09:14.110
It's great to be here.

00:09:14.110 --> 00:09:19.329
Google is founded on a mission to organize
the world's information and make it universally

00:09:19.329 --> 00:09:20.540
accessible and useful.

00:09:20.540 --> 00:09:27.550
For many years, we've had the privilege to
create products that have served billions

00:09:27.550 --> 00:09:31.089
of consumers across the world.

00:09:31.089 --> 00:09:36.600
To do that, we've had to engage pretty much
every branch of computer science.

00:09:36.600 --> 00:09:38.480
And we've had breakthroughs along the way.

00:09:38.480 --> 00:09:46.459
It has led us to breakthroughs in cloud computing
technology, collaboration software, and analytics,

00:09:46.459 --> 00:09:50.149
things which are now transforming businesses
everywhere.

00:09:50.149 --> 00:09:59.339
So with Google Cloud, now we offer businesses,
Google-quality computing, security, and business

00:09:59.339 --> 00:10:00.379
tools.

00:10:00.379 --> 00:10:06.190
And it is an extraordinary big bet for us,
both as a platform internally for our own

00:10:06.190 --> 00:10:13.670
innovation but also as a way of bringing our
infrastructure to serve the needs of businesses

00:10:13.670 --> 00:10:16.320
across the world.

00:10:16.320 --> 00:10:22.939
Our customers and partners will see many ways
to leverage all of Google, be it Google Maps,

00:10:22.939 --> 00:10:27.120
Android, YouTube, our devices ecosystem.

00:10:27.120 --> 00:10:33.230
And we'll deliver it over the most efficient,
high-performing, and secure core computing

00:10:33.230 --> 00:10:34.230
resources.

00:10:34.230 --> 00:10:41.370
For over 15 years, we have also been investing
in machine learning and AI.

00:10:41.370 --> 00:10:47.959
And as businesses increasingly shift online
and create more information, they can leverage

00:10:47.959 --> 00:10:54.480
these AI and machine learning tools to understand
the information and build better products

00:10:54.480 --> 00:10:56.839
that delight more customers.

00:10:56.839 --> 00:11:02.790
Above all, we are excited to see what you
all will create with our products and services.

00:11:02.790 --> 00:11:09.939
When we ship things on the consumer side,
we always get surprised in how they use our

00:11:09.939 --> 00:11:12.740
technology in a thousand unexpected ways.

00:11:12.740 --> 00:11:15.690
It makes us better as a company.

00:11:15.690 --> 00:11:18.209
With Google Cloud, we hope to have the same
journey.

00:11:18.209 --> 00:11:21.899
We hope to grow and learn alongside you.

00:11:21.899 --> 00:11:28.490
Partners already have pushed us to add more
ways to collaborate beyond video conferencing.

00:11:28.490 --> 00:11:31.769
They've exposed us to more real-world scaling
needs.

00:11:31.769 --> 00:11:36.110
For example, the demand that retailers like
Home Depot get on Black Friday.

00:11:36.110 --> 00:11:40.959
They've shown us exciting new use cases for
Google products.

00:11:40.959 --> 00:11:46.360
You will see what eBay has done with Google
Home as an example.

00:11:46.360 --> 00:11:51.529
In many ways, to me Google Cloud is a natural
extension of our mission to make information

00:11:51.529 --> 00:11:53.389
accessible and useful.

00:11:53.389 --> 00:11:55.509
We are just doing it for businesses.

00:11:55.509 --> 00:12:02.819
It means increasing the availability and lowering
the cost of data storage and computing resources.

00:12:02.819 --> 00:12:08.589
It means making it easier for businesses to
understand and act on critical information.

00:12:08.589 --> 00:12:10.190
That's what G Suite does.

00:12:10.190 --> 00:12:13.750
G Suite enhances information sharing and collaboration.

00:12:13.750 --> 00:12:21.420
Machine learning finds previously hidden patterns
in data, new information which helps businesses

00:12:21.420 --> 00:12:24.300
improve their products and better serve their
customers.

00:12:24.300 --> 00:12:30.139
So I'm really excited about the progress here,
and I can't wait to see what you all build

00:12:30.139 --> 00:12:31.139
next.

00:12:31.139 --> 00:12:35.339
So without further delay, I'm going to invite
Diane back on to the stage.

00:12:35.339 --> 00:12:41.769
She joined us in late 2015 and the business
has grown dramatically for us under her leadership.

00:12:41.769 --> 00:12:45.230
And given that it's International Women's
Day, it's also a pleasure to welcome back

00:12:45.230 --> 00:12:49.089
on stage a trailblazer for women in technology.

00:12:49.089 --> 00:12:53.790
So let's have Diane back to share more about
how Google Cloud is helping all of you.

00:12:53.790 --> 00:12:56.370
Thank you.

00:12:56.370 --> 00:13:06.579
[ Applause ]
Diane Greene: Thank you so much, Sundar.

00:13:06.579 --> 00:13:09.600
We really are One Google.

00:13:09.600 --> 00:13:14.970
And at Cloud, you know, one of the reasons
I joined, we're leveraging almost two decades

00:13:14.970 --> 00:13:21.209
of innovation and technology, really built
for the kind of enterprises we have today

00:13:21.209 --> 00:13:24.990
or we're starting to have.

00:13:24.990 --> 00:13:30.129
I want to just kind of underscore or say a
few words about our infrastructure.

00:13:30.129 --> 00:13:37.120
We don't really normally give out many numbers,
and I'm not going to necessarily break that

00:13:37.120 --> 00:13:38.120
policy.

00:13:38.120 --> 00:13:40.730
But I am going to tell you some interesting
nuggets, okay?

00:13:40.730 --> 00:13:46.389
We have possibly the world's biggest, most
powerful, secure network.

00:13:46.389 --> 00:13:52.499
Tomorrow Urs Holzle, who is employee number
eight, behind our data centers and infrastructure

00:13:52.499 --> 00:14:01.220
is going to go into this more and tell you
about what we're doing and where we're going.

00:14:01.220 --> 00:14:09.029
But today what I'm going to tell you about
is just the kind of the scale of our infrastructure.

00:14:09.029 --> 00:14:17.579
If you took not even our largest data center
and stacked all the servers one after another,

00:14:17.579 --> 00:14:23.029
it would be higher than more than 5,000 feet
above Mount Everest.

00:14:23.029 --> 00:14:24.029
Okay?

00:14:24.029 --> 00:14:28.589
I was just going to say above Mount Everest,
but the person that calculated this said no,

00:14:28.589 --> 00:14:31.329
no, add the 5,000 feet.

00:14:31.329 --> 00:14:36.110
[ Laughter ]
Just one building in our South Carolina campus

00:14:36.110 --> 00:14:41.970
is bigger than Stanford's Arrillaga football
stadium.

00:14:41.970 --> 00:14:44.940
We have a new complex in the Netherlands.

00:14:44.940 --> 00:14:48.120
It's got over 10,000 miles of cable.

00:14:48.120 --> 00:14:52.259
That's enough to go from here to Tokyo and
back.

00:14:52.259 --> 00:14:58.860
And then one of the things we're all just
very proud of at Google is we've been focused

00:14:58.860 --> 00:15:02.160
on being carbon neutral from the beginning.

00:15:02.160 --> 00:15:04.570
You know, compute takes a lot of power.

00:15:04.570 --> 00:15:09.519
Some days we will be as efficient as our brains,
but it does take a lot of power.

00:15:09.519 --> 00:15:13.649
Google is the largest purchaser of renewable
energy.

00:15:13.649 --> 00:15:14.779
That's wind farms.

00:15:14.779 --> 00:15:15.779
That's solar.

00:15:15.779 --> 00:15:25.509
In fact, our purchases are larger than the
next three or four purchasers combined.

00:15:25.509 --> 00:15:32.600
[ Applause ]
So why did I tell you about that scale?

00:15:32.600 --> 00:15:35.209
Well, I want to give you some examples of
why it matters.

00:15:35.209 --> 00:15:42.980
Just two customers, you couldn't have failed
to notice that Niantic Labs launched Pokemon

00:15:42.980 --> 00:15:45.279
Go earlier this year.

00:15:45.279 --> 00:15:52.649
And just six days after they released, they
kind of eclipsed their most optimistic three-month

00:15:52.649 --> 00:15:54.160
predictions.

00:15:54.160 --> 00:15:57.069
Their queries increased fifty-fold.

00:15:57.069 --> 00:15:59.499
So they were running on Google Cloud.

00:15:59.499 --> 00:16:05.920
And our site reliability engineers were with
them 24 hours a day, seven days a week making

00:16:05.920 --> 00:16:08.180
this thing go flawlessly.

00:16:08.180 --> 00:16:10.129
They were there around the clock.

00:16:10.129 --> 00:16:16.559
In fact, it's when we realized we needed the
same kind of functionality for our customers.

00:16:16.559 --> 00:16:23.759
Pokemon Go last week at the World Congress
announced -- Niantic announced they had 88

00:16:23.759 --> 00:16:28.709
billion Pokemon caught.

00:16:28.709 --> 00:16:30.709
Painless scaling on Google Cloud.

00:16:30.709 --> 00:16:36.930
The other example is an enterprise customer,
very innovative retailer, Home Depot.

00:16:36.930 --> 00:16:40.300
They're now running on Google Cloud.

00:16:40.300 --> 00:16:42.160
And we got them through Black Friday.

00:16:42.160 --> 00:16:45.649
It was the most painless Black Friday ever.

00:16:45.649 --> 00:16:50.649
And they will be here later today to talk
about it.

00:16:50.649 --> 00:16:57.829
Also with that scale comes the need for phenomenal
security.

00:16:57.829 --> 00:17:01.920
And I'm just going to go into that a little.

00:17:01.920 --> 00:17:05.270
This shows sort of the layers of security.

00:17:05.270 --> 00:17:12.880
And you can see there's many gaps between
the networks, the servers and the software.

00:17:12.880 --> 00:17:17.299
So we built our own chips in the network and
the computing devices.

00:17:17.299 --> 00:17:21.779
We have to look at every single endpoint.

00:17:21.779 --> 00:17:27.169
We actually have over 700 people just full-time
worrying about security, watching the networks,

00:17:27.169 --> 00:17:29.630
securing Google Cloud.

00:17:29.630 --> 00:17:36.640
And if you add a Chromebook, which diskless
Chrome -- doesn't store, you get an added

00:17:36.640 --> 00:17:38.550
layer of security endpoint.

00:17:38.550 --> 00:17:42.930
I personally only use a Chromebook.

00:17:42.930 --> 00:17:51.130
By the way, Chromebooks last year outsold
Mac OS in the Macs in the United States.

00:17:51.130 --> 00:17:54.539
And something we're really proud of is what
we're doing for education.

00:17:54.539 --> 00:18:04.860
Over 20 million Chromebooks used by students
in schools worldwide and growing.

00:18:04.860 --> 00:18:13.250
[ Applause ]
So cloud is just a transformational technology.

00:18:13.250 --> 00:18:19.159
It's changing how people architect their information,
use their data.

00:18:19.159 --> 00:18:22.980
It's changing how they work with their customers,
how they work together.

00:18:22.980 --> 00:18:28.960
You know, in effect, our companies are becoming
virtualized.

00:18:28.960 --> 00:18:32.149
We're operating both in the physical world
and the virtual, and there's many things you

00:18:32.149 --> 00:18:37.510
can do in the virtual world more powerfully
than you can in the physical, particularly

00:18:37.510 --> 00:18:46.630
as we all start taking advantage of machine
learning.

00:18:46.630 --> 00:18:54.730
You know, I really believe that the cloud
with the best technology is the best cloud.

00:18:54.730 --> 00:18:57.480
It gives you a competitive edge.

00:18:57.480 --> 00:19:02.980
And you want to be on a cloud that's going
to keep you on the edge of -- technology is

00:19:02.980 --> 00:19:08.270
moving so fast, and you want to maintain that
competitive advantage.

00:19:08.270 --> 00:19:14.190
But all this technology needs phenomenal customer
focus.

00:19:14.190 --> 00:19:20.110
We've really doubled down on our customer-facing
part of Google.

00:19:20.110 --> 00:19:24.919
And I'm super -- you know, as I said, super
proud today to give you those proof points,

00:19:24.919 --> 00:19:31.230
to have our customers here to tell you about
that, customers and partners.

00:19:31.230 --> 00:19:34.090
