for people.

00:36:04.280 --> 00:36:08.480
You know, really increase customer satisfaction.

00:36:08.480 --> 00:36:16.220
And just to underscore that, we have a joint
customer, just a wonderful person, and from

00:36:16.220 --> 00:36:23.300
a company that's actually 211 years old, I'm
really pleased to welcome Mike Crowe, the

00:36:23.300 --> 00:36:27.719
chief information officer from Colgate-Palmolive,
if he can join us on stage.

00:36:27.719 --> 00:36:32.020
[ Applause ]
Bernd Leukert: By the way, a long-lasting

00:36:32.020 --> 00:36:33.540
friend of mine.

00:36:33.540 --> 00:36:35.569
Diane Greene: Hi, Mike.

00:36:35.569 --> 00:36:36.589
Thank you.

00:36:36.589 --> 00:36:38.619
Mike Crowe: Good morning.

00:36:38.619 --> 00:36:41.660
Bernd Leukert: Good to see you.

00:36:41.660 --> 00:36:43.849
Diane Greene: Great to have you here, Mike.

00:36:43.849 --> 00:36:48.470
Please, why don't you just tell us a little
bit about Colgate-Palmolive and your partnerships

00:36:48.470 --> 00:36:49.470
with both of us.

00:36:49.470 --> 00:36:50.470
Mike Crowe: Oh, sure.

00:36:50.470 --> 00:36:51.770
So, first of all, thanks for having me.

00:36:51.770 --> 00:36:53.030
I'm happy to be here.

00:36:53.030 --> 00:36:59.000
Colgate is a leading global consumer products
company, selling our products in 223 countries

00:36:59.000 --> 00:37:00.830
around the world.

00:37:00.830 --> 00:37:08.180
Our 38,000 Colgate people focus on four core
categories: oral care, personal care, home

00:37:08.180 --> 00:37:10.400
care, and pet nutrition.

00:37:10.400 --> 00:37:12.740
Likewise, we have a very focused I.T.

00:37:12.740 --> 00:37:19.250
strategy which at its core relies on a small
number of strategic partnerships.

00:37:19.250 --> 00:37:27.460
We chose SAP 23 years ago, and Google Cloud
just last year, for very much the same reasons.

00:37:27.460 --> 00:37:32.040
You're both highly innovative and you both
have a willingness to partner with us.

00:37:32.040 --> 00:37:35.880
And we see tremendous value from implementing
your technology.

00:37:35.880 --> 00:37:39.730
We've, of course, been innovating with SAP
for years on business processes and we're

00:37:39.730 --> 00:37:44.230
already starting to see the benefit of Google's
collaboration suite of applications.

00:37:44.230 --> 00:37:49.450
Diane Greene: So Mike, I mean, you were one
of the first potential customers I met when

00:37:49.450 --> 00:37:53.760
I joined Google, and now you've implemented
G Suite.

00:37:53.760 --> 00:37:55.430
Can you tell us a little bit about it?

00:37:55.430 --> 00:37:56.599
Mike Crowe: Sure.

00:37:56.599 --> 00:38:05.010
So we started our project in May of last year
and we were very encouraged by the positive

00:38:05.010 --> 00:38:11.210
email responses we received from our employees
as we announced that we were Going Google.

00:38:11.210 --> 00:38:16.830
Still, we knew that it would be a major change
management effort to go to a new collaboration

00:38:16.830 --> 00:38:22.520
platform, so Diane, we worked with your team
as well as our implementation partner, SADA

00:38:22.520 --> 00:38:27.609
Systems, to lay out a phased approach for
implementation, and in just three months we

00:38:27.609 --> 00:38:30.750
were able to launch for our global I.T.

00:38:30.750 --> 00:38:33.310
organization all around the world.

00:38:33.310 --> 00:38:38.180
We learned from that and then we leveraged
that excitement that our employees had shown

00:38:38.180 --> 00:38:42.130
to recruit a few thousand volunteers to be
early adopters.

00:38:42.130 --> 00:38:48.300
And within that set, we had about 900 Google
guides who were to be the champions on the

00:38:48.300 --> 00:38:52.810
ground as we implemented and to be the trainers
as we rolled out.

00:38:52.810 --> 00:38:57.579
And all of that set us up very nicely for
our final implementation, which was in one

00:38:57.579 --> 00:39:01.210
weekend in November we took more than 20,000
users live.

00:39:01.210 --> 00:39:04.410
So all told, more than 28,000 users in six
months.

00:39:04.410 --> 00:39:05.750
Diane Greene: Yeah, Mike.

00:39:05.750 --> 00:39:11.370
I mean, it's just phenomenal to successfully
move 23,000 people over the weekend, and I

00:39:11.370 --> 00:39:15.849
can't tell you how much I enjoyed the little
video you sent me to educate your company

00:39:15.849 --> 00:39:20.130
where you're sitting there talking to Mr.
C, a cartoon character.

00:39:20.130 --> 00:39:22.100
What have you seen since you launched?

00:39:22.100 --> 00:39:26.890
Mike Crowe: Well, in just a little over three
months, we see that people are working differently.

00:39:26.890 --> 00:39:33.580
Sure, everybody is using mail and calendar,
but the usage of the other tools is quite

00:39:33.580 --> 00:39:34.839
impressive.

00:39:34.839 --> 00:39:43.400
90% of our users are active on Google Drive,
and in February alone, we had more than 57,000

00:39:43.400 --> 00:39:45.000
hours of video Hangouts.

00:39:45.000 --> 00:39:50.819
You know, allowing our user base to connect
more easily both while in the office as well

00:39:50.819 --> 00:39:53.940
as on mobile devices while outside the office.

00:39:53.940 --> 00:39:57.690
And this is critically important for us as
our Colgate teams around the world work more

00:39:57.690 --> 00:40:01.670
and more together from different locations.

00:40:01.670 --> 00:40:07.510
And then finally, I'm seeing a faster uptake
in the productivity suites, in docs and sheets

00:40:07.510 --> 00:40:08.930
and slides as well.

00:40:08.930 --> 00:40:09.930
Faster than I had expected.

00:40:09.930 --> 00:40:16.069
Bernd Leukert: So Mike, I still remember when
you asked me last summer, "How is your relationship

00:40:16.069 --> 00:40:17.069
to Google?"

00:40:17.069 --> 00:40:20.880
And I was thinking we have conversation, now
he is asking me, he knows me now for more

00:40:20.880 --> 00:40:23.319
than two decades, but now we are here together.

00:40:23.319 --> 00:40:25.090
I think it's pretty amazing.

00:40:25.090 --> 00:40:31.119
But I have to ask you: Now we have made the
first step, but what excites you going forward?

00:40:31.119 --> 00:40:33.869
What should the two of us deliver to you going
forward?

00:40:33.869 --> 00:40:37.579
Mike Crowe: Well, you know, first I'm very
excited about the Google/SAP partnership,

00:40:37.579 --> 00:40:42.369
about the announcements that have been made
here today, and about the possibilities that

00:40:42.369 --> 00:40:47.720
the combined strengths of these two great
companies bring out.

00:40:47.720 --> 00:40:53.720
I think it makes perfect sense, it's a perfect
fit, to combine SAP's business -- enterprise

00:40:53.720 --> 00:40:59.349
business application expertise with Google's
expertise on Infrastructure-as-a-Service running

00:40:59.349 --> 00:41:00.730
in the Google Cloud platform.

00:41:00.730 --> 00:41:07.339
Bernd, you and I have been talking about incorporating
machine learning into a broad array of the

00:41:07.339 --> 00:41:10.280
SAP business processes for some time, so excited
about that as well.

00:41:10.280 --> 00:41:11.280
Bernd Leukert: Yes.

00:41:11.280 --> 00:41:16.660
Mike Crowe: And then one more example, as
you know, we're users of SAP's Digital Boardroom,

00:41:16.660 --> 00:41:21.349
a co-innovation project between us and SAP
which allows us to review the state of the

00:41:21.349 --> 00:41:27.150
business at any time using the latest data,
and we're already working on incorporating

00:41:27.150 --> 00:41:30.109
Google slides into that application as well.

00:41:30.109 --> 00:41:33.990
So in summary, I think the possibilities are
going to be numerous for us.

00:41:33.990 --> 00:41:37.220
Diane Greene: This has been really terrific
for us.

00:41:37.220 --> 00:41:39.880
We've really enjoyed working with both of
you.

00:41:39.880 --> 00:41:43.690
It's great for Google Cloud, it's great for
you guys, and we're really looking forward

00:41:43.690 --> 00:41:46.069
to see what we're all going to do next.

00:41:46.069 --> 00:41:47.500
Thank you so much, both of you.

00:41:47.500 --> 00:41:49.000
Bernd Leukert: Thanks for having us here,
Diane.

00:41:49.000 --> 00:41:50.190
Diane Greene: Yeah.

00:41:50.190 --> 00:41:51.190
All right.

00:41:51.190 --> 00:41:52.960
Bernd Leukert: Thanks a lot.

00:41:52.960 --> 00:41:55.730
Diane Greene: Thanks, guys, that was great.

00:41:55.730 --> 00:41:56.920
Mike Crowe: Thanks.

00:41:56.920 --> 00:42:04.559
Diane Greene: As Mike was saying, G Suite
is this fantastic collaboration suite.

00:42:04.559 --> 00:42:11.710
It's really transforming the culture and the
productivity at Colgate.

00:42:11.710 --> 00:42:15.299
One thing also that I'll just add about G
Suite that we hear in every customer that

00:42:15.299 --> 00:42:22.089
deploys is the young people so rejoice when
G Suite gets rolled out.

00:42:22.089 --> 00:42:29.620
And one of our bigger customers, PwC, actually
did a poll of their employee base.

00:42:29.620 --> 00:42:36.840
And the number one valued thing from particularly
their young employees was their collaboration

00:42:36.840 --> 00:42:39.010
and productivity tool.

00:42:39.010 --> 00:42:42.980
It's so important to them.

00:42:42.980 --> 00:42:45.010
Okay.

00:42:45.010 --> 00:42:51.950
So for our next guest, I'm really excited
to bring out a premiere technology company,

00:42:51.950 --> 00:42:52.960
Verizon.

00:42:52.960 --> 00:42:59.210
They have got 114 million retail customers.

00:42:59.210 --> 00:43:07.290
We've been working closely with Verizon for
a year now, not simply -- and we've been working

00:43:07.290 --> 00:43:11.260
with them on workplace productivity.

00:43:11.260 --> 00:43:12.670
It's been a long project.

00:43:12.670 --> 00:43:15.220
It's been a challenging project.

00:43:15.220 --> 00:43:19.200
And that's -- I really wanted to talk about
that here.

00:43:19.200 --> 00:43:21.290
It's gone really well, great outcome.

00:43:21.290 --> 00:43:25.960
And here to talk about it coming on stage
let me welcome Alin D'Silva who is the vice

00:43:25.960 --> 00:43:28.960
president for digital workplace at Verizon.

00:43:28.960 --> 00:43:31.420
[ Applause ]
Hi, Alin.

00:43:31.420 --> 00:43:35.510
Thanks so much for coming.

00:43:35.510 --> 00:43:38.780
Great to see you.

00:43:38.780 --> 00:43:42.869
Alin D'Silva: Good morning, everyone.

00:43:42.869 --> 00:43:49.430
At Verizon, we believe deeply in our brand
statement which says, "Better Matters."

00:43:49.430 --> 00:43:55.079
And we believe better matters not just for
our customers but our employees as well.

00:43:55.079 --> 00:44:02.339
We are keenly aware of the need to move faster,
collaborate more effectively, have flexibility

00:44:02.339 --> 00:44:05.410
in how they work, when and where.

00:44:05.410 --> 00:44:11.930
And it's really a commitment we've made to
a much more modern and progressive work environment.

00:44:11.930 --> 00:44:14.990
And we're delighted that G Suite is going
to be a big part of that transformation.

00:44:14.990 --> 00:44:15.991
Diane Greene: So are we.

00:44:15.991 --> 00:44:20.220
We are delighted, too, Alin.

00:44:20.220 --> 00:44:24.770
You know, starting in just a few weeks, I
understand you're going to start moving 150,000

00:44:24.770 --> 00:44:29.030
Verizon workers over to the GC platform.

00:44:29.030 --> 00:44:33.099
But we both know it didn't happen that quickly,
that you started down this path more than

00:44:33.099 --> 00:44:34.099
a year ago.

00:44:34.099 --> 00:44:36.079
And I would just like to know, what were you
looking for?

00:44:36.079 --> 00:44:37.079
Alin D'Silva: Yeah.

00:44:37.079 --> 00:44:41.910
So a little over a year ago we formed a group
that was really focused on raising the bar

00:44:41.910 --> 00:44:47.950
in productivity through collaboration and
employee engagement.

00:44:47.950 --> 00:44:50.530
And we've done a lot in that short period
of time.

00:44:50.530 --> 00:44:54.540
We fostered collaboration by rolling out activity-based
workplaces.

00:44:54.540 --> 00:44:56.730
We rolled out unified communications globally.

00:44:56.730 --> 00:45:01.380
And we made improvements to our mobile options.

00:45:01.380 --> 00:45:07.130
We realized we were really missing some key
features, things like realtime collaboration

00:45:07.130 --> 00:45:11.410
and other modern features that you see in
these productivity suites.

00:45:11.410 --> 00:45:16.849
So we did a comprehensive trial of G Suite,
and our employees really loved it.

00:45:16.849 --> 00:45:22.339
They loved the simplicity and intuitiveness
of the product, the ease of use.

00:45:22.339 --> 00:45:25.290
The realtime collaboration really blew them
away.

00:45:25.290 --> 00:45:28.670
You know, it worked well on desktops.

00:45:28.670 --> 00:45:32.740
What they were really surprised by was how
well it worked on mobile devices.

00:45:32.740 --> 00:45:38.250
And, you know, the simplicity of the solution
actually helped us secure the platform in

00:45:38.250 --> 00:45:41.100
a much more straightforward way.

00:45:41.100 --> 00:45:44.410
And then I would say the last thing is that
we realized that actually a number of our

00:45:44.410 --> 00:45:50.109
employees at Verizon, specifically at companies
like AOL and Telogis were already on G Suite.

00:45:50.109 --> 00:45:55.250
And so for us that's how we came about to
make the decision.

00:45:55.250 --> 00:46:02.400
We realized we had to escape to where the
puck is going because in terms of talent,

00:46:02.400 --> 00:46:07.309
we knew, you know, as the years passed, the
talent that comes into the company is going

00:46:07.309 --> 00:46:09.540
to expect a product like this.

00:46:09.540 --> 00:46:12.890
And so we had that in mind as we made our
decisioning.

00:46:12.890 --> 00:46:15.089
Diane Greene: Thank you.

00:46:15.089 --> 00:46:19.700
You know, Alin, a lot of the time, changes
-- change can be pretty hard no matter how

00:46:19.700 --> 00:46:22.160
good the technology is.

00:46:22.160 --> 00:46:25.380
And did you have resistance in going to the
cloud?

00:46:25.380 --> 00:46:28.030
And what did you do about that, if you did
have that resistance?

00:46:28.030 --> 00:46:30.840
Alin D'Silva: Sure, we had a fair amount of
resistance.

00:46:30.840 --> 00:46:35.130
But it is really a matter of addressing the
fundamental perceptions of what it takes to

00:46:35.130 --> 00:46:36.380
go to the cloud.

00:46:36.380 --> 00:46:39.230
So what we did is we cracked open the door.

00:46:39.230 --> 00:46:42.210
We asked everyone to sort of keep an open
mind.

00:46:42.210 --> 00:46:46.860
And then over several months, you know, we
got really deep on a number of topics like

00:46:46.860 --> 00:46:55.069
security, operations, user experience, regulation,
and compliance matters.

00:46:55.069 --> 00:46:57.760
And we also looked closely at ROI, of course.

00:46:57.760 --> 00:47:02.840
And in going through the trial, in talking
to you, in talking to your partners, in talking

00:47:02.840 --> 00:47:07.470
to your customers, we realized that really
it was the right decision for us.

00:47:07.470 --> 00:47:10.230
The Google team was really fantastic to work
with.

00:47:10.230 --> 00:47:14.299
Eric Geithner, if you're out there, kudos
to you.

00:47:14.299 --> 00:47:16.710
But they answered all of our questions.

00:47:16.710 --> 00:47:20.260
You know, they got us really comfortable with
the decision that we needed to make.

00:47:20.260 --> 00:47:23.010
Diane Greene: Thanks for that shout-out to
Eric.

00:47:23.010 --> 00:47:27.789
Alin, do you have any advice for anyone in
the audience that's looking at making a similar

00:47:27.789 --> 00:47:30.660
transformation that you've gone through at
Verizon?

00:47:30.660 --> 00:47:32.130
Alin D'Silva: Absolutely.

00:47:32.130 --> 00:47:35.510
So, first of all, you've got to bring along
all the stakeholders that are going to be

00:47:35.510 --> 00:47:37.640
key in the decision-making that you're going
to make.

00:47:37.640 --> 00:47:43.640
So bring along your CISO and legal teams for
starters for sure because they need to go

00:47:43.640 --> 00:47:44.790
on this journey with everyone.

00:47:44.790 --> 00:47:50.170
They have got to understand what it's going
to take and get comfortable with it.

00:47:50.170 --> 00:47:56.640
The other thing we found is we learned a lot
from customers of Google and G Suite and from

00:47:56.640 --> 00:47:57.640
partners.

00:47:57.640 --> 00:47:59.860
So I would say definitely take advantage of
that.

00:47:59.860 --> 00:48:04.950
Remember that this is, like, partly -- I mean,
it's a technology transformation, but it's

00:48:04.950 --> 00:48:08.700
really about change and driving change in
the organization.

00:48:08.700 --> 00:48:12.890
So you've really got to give thought to how
you're going to drive change management around

00:48:12.890 --> 00:48:13.890
this.

00:48:13.890 --> 00:48:18.770
And I would say, like, you know, if your mission
is to go transform the company, then you really

00:48:18.770 --> 00:48:20.559
have to have meaningful change.

00:48:20.559 --> 00:48:22.109
Otherwise, like, where's the transformation?

00:48:22.109 --> 00:48:24.299
Diane Greene: Right.

00:48:24.299 --> 00:48:28.940
So what has the reception been like since
you announced this move to G Suite?

00:48:28.940 --> 00:48:31.170
Alin D'Silva: The reception has been pretty
interesting.

00:48:31.170 --> 00:48:36.700
So we put out an article on our Internet site
in the middle of January.

00:48:36.700 --> 00:48:40.360
And within a couple of days, it got the most
reads for the whole month.

00:48:40.360 --> 00:48:44.150
We had hundreds of comments and questions
that were posted.

00:48:44.150 --> 00:48:49.220
And, you know, people saw it as a really meaningful
step in the right direction.

00:48:49.220 --> 00:48:54.660
These were folks who had experienced G Suite
at other jobs or had used it in their personal

00:48:54.660 --> 00:48:55.660
lives.

00:48:55.660 --> 00:48:59.730
And they were really comfortable and excited
about the idea that we would go forward in

00:48:59.730 --> 00:49:00.960
this direction.

00:49:00.960 --> 00:49:05.069
And then there were a number of employees
who, you know, naturally were concerned about

00:49:05.069 --> 00:49:06.720
how it was going to change their job.

00:49:06.720 --> 00:49:08.920
They were used to doing things a certain way.

00:49:08.920 --> 00:49:10.569
And they posted a lot of questions online.

00:49:10.569 --> 00:49:13.770
And we went through and answered each and
every one of them.

00:49:13.770 --> 00:49:18.450
And that really helped the broader community
understand where we were going.

00:49:18.450 --> 00:49:21.099
And that was really important.

00:49:21.099 --> 00:49:25.810
We also identified all the early enthusiasts,
the ones -- we call them Google guides, just

00:49:25.810 --> 00:49:26.920
like Mike said earlier.

00:49:26.920 --> 00:49:33.210
We are going to rope them into early adoption
phases of this so they can get on board and

00:49:33.210 --> 00:49:36.770
get ready to support our colleagues when we
bring them overall.

00:49:36.770 --> 00:49:39.510
So, you know, it was a great validation.

00:49:39.510 --> 00:49:43.329
It was a great early validation that we made
the right choice, and we are really looking

00:49:43.329 --> 00:49:45.910
forward to starting the migrations in a few
weeks.

00:49:45.910 --> 00:49:48.049
Diane Greene: You know, it's a big step, Alin.

00:49:48.049 --> 00:49:51.710
And I really admire the commitment you brought
to bear on this.

00:49:51.710 --> 00:49:53.430
We have been working pretty closely.

00:49:53.430 --> 00:49:59.710
I know I will be hearing more from you, and
I really look forward to the updates and supporting

00:49:59.710 --> 00:50:00.710
you.

00:50:00.710 --> 00:50:01.710
Alin D'Silva: Great.

00:50:01.710 --> 00:50:02.710
Thanks, Diane.

00:50:02.710 --> 00:50:03.710
Diane Greene: Thank you.

00:50:03.710 --> 00:50:04.710
Thanks so much.

00:50:04.710 --> 00:50:05.710
Bye-bye.

00:50:05.710 --> 00:50:10.849
[ Applause ]
Yeah, G Suite, you know, is built for the

00:50:10.849 --> 00:50:14.780
cloud, integrated, works seamlessly.

00:50:14.780 --> 00:50:19.550
It's really proving its value.

00:50:19.550 --> 00:50:25.020
Going back to Google Cloud, once someone decides
to move to Google Cloud, you know, we really

00:50:25.020 --> 00:50:30.599
have an incredible commitment to deliver top-quality
customer support.

00:50:30.599 --> 00:50:32.660
We don't want any of our customers going down.

00:50:32.660 --> 00:50:40.170
You know, we use site reliability engineers
to keep our seven apps that have over a billion

00:50:40.170 --> 00:50:42.700
active users available.

00:50:42.700 --> 00:50:48.530
And we realized, as I said before, with the
Niantic Pokemon Go launch that our customers

00:50:48.530 --> 00:50:49.530
need the same.

00:50:49.530 --> 00:50:55.450
We established a new organization called customer
reliability engineering services where those

00:50:55.450 --> 00:51:04.250
people work with our customers to make sure
that your services are architected and monitored

00:51:04.250 --> 00:51:06.700
and keep full availability.

00:51:06.700 --> 00:51:17.530
Now, Google can't scale to do enough customer
reliability engineers for all of our customers.

00:51:17.530 --> 00:51:25.950
And so what we've done is we've reached out
to partners, and I'm really excited today

00:51:25.950 --> 00:51:30.099
to announce two of our early partners.

00:51:30.099 --> 00:51:39.670
We have Pivotal Labs, a premiere consulting
development company leading in agile development,

00:51:39.670 --> 00:51:44.380
our first certified CRE partner.

00:51:44.380 --> 00:51:49.180
They offer CRE services to our customer.

00:51:49.180 --> 00:51:54.220
And then another customer, really a pioneer
in the cloud, they understand the cloud inside

00:51:54.220 --> 00:51:57.849
out, that's Rackspace.

00:51:57.849 --> 00:52:01.849
And they are our first managed service provider,
and we look forward to doing more and more

00:52:01.849 --> 00:52:05.849
with them.

00:52:05.849 --> 00:52:13.030
Now somebody that's already taken advantage
of our CRE, Home Depot.

00:52:13.030 --> 00:52:19.069
I'm really excited to welcome to the stage
Paul Gaffney, senior vice president at Home

00:52:19.069 --> 00:52:20.069
Depot.

00:52:20.069 --> 00:52:22.799
[ Applause ]
Hi, Paul.

00:52:22.799 --> 00:52:25.599
Thanks so much.

00:52:25.599 --> 00:52:31.200
Paul Gaffney: Great to see you.

00:52:31.200 --> 00:52:35.309
Diane Greene: So you have got an impressive
history.

00:52:35.309 --> 00:52:38.839
Quite an innovative retailer doing extremely
well.

00:52:38.839 --> 00:52:41.020
How do you think about technology?

00:52:41.020 --> 00:52:46.380
Paul Gaffney: Technology is a really important
thing to us, particularly in this landscape

00:52:46.380 --> 00:52:53.720
as consumers' expectations are increasingly
being shaped by the software that they use.

00:52:53.720 --> 00:53:00.549
We've got over 2,000 stores in North America
and 400,000 real, live human beings who take

00:53:00.549 --> 00:53:01.960
care of our customers.

00:53:01.960 --> 00:53:07.170
And that's our heritage, that excellent experience
that you get when you're talking to someone

00:53:07.170 --> 00:53:08.740
wearing an orange apron.

00:53:08.740 --> 00:53:13.329
And increasingly consumers are expecting that
we do exactly that same thing in software.

00:53:13.329 --> 00:53:17.780
So we think about software as an extension
of our overall culture.

00:53:17.780 --> 00:53:20.690
Diane Greene: It's sort of your virtual company
running, isn't it?

00:53:20.690 --> 00:53:22.010
Paul Gaffney: It is.

00:53:22.010 --> 00:53:26.650
Diane Greene: And what have been the proof
points in using the cloud for you?

00:53:26.650 --> 00:53:33.200
Paul Gaffney: The important thing about cloud
for us, as we started to get better as a software

00:53:33.200 --> 00:53:38.670
engineering organization, we paid attention
to a lot of different things.

00:53:38.670 --> 00:53:41.539
One of those things was building cloud-native
apps.

00:53:41.539 --> 00:53:45.440
There were a variety of reasons why we focused
on building cloud-native apps.

00:53:45.440 --> 00:53:48.890
But we also said, well, we should probably
run them on the cloud if we're going to build

00:53:48.890 --> 00:53:51.099
them cloud native.

00:53:51.099 --> 00:53:56.000
And cloud plays an important role for us economically.

00:53:56.000 --> 00:54:02.010
Cloud economics are a substantial improvement
to our on-premises economics but also thinking

00:54:02.010 --> 00:54:07.050
differently about resiliency and availability.

00:54:07.050 --> 00:54:12.800
Moving from, I think, what's a classical big
company I.T.

00:54:12.800 --> 00:54:20.440
orientation, which is make the infrastructure
robust, to a more modern view that says, just

00:54:20.440 --> 00:54:23.369
assume that everything is going to fail.

00:54:23.369 --> 00:54:29.440
And that was a big catalyst for us to engineer
our software differently and partner with

00:54:29.440 --> 00:54:33.539
you guys on learning what does it mean to
live in a world where you make your stuff

00:54:33.539 --> 00:54:34.680
resilient to failure.

00:54:34.680 --> 00:54:40.079
Diane Greene: You know, I've come to love
all these days when you have these spike loads.

00:54:40.079 --> 00:54:44.560
And you guys recently, you know -- of course,
every year you have Black Friday.

00:54:44.560 --> 00:54:47.020
I was just wondering, how did it go for you
guys this year?

00:54:47.020 --> 00:54:50.460
Paul Gaffney: I'm glad you love these spike
loads.

00:54:50.460 --> 00:54:52.970
[ Laughter ]
And as shareholders --

00:54:52.970 --> 00:54:54.309
Diane Greene: I bite my fingernails.

00:54:54.309 --> 00:54:55.440
No, not really.

00:54:55.440 --> 00:54:57.329
Paul Gaffney: So do we.

00:54:57.329 --> 00:55:02.110
As everyone in here is a consumer, I think
you guys all know there are times during the

00:55:02.110 --> 00:55:10.319
year when all of us in retail attempt to conspire
to get you to do more shopping than is normal.

00:55:10.319 --> 00:55:14.800
But those of you who are in the room who know
that that ends up somewhere, that has to be

00:55:14.800 --> 00:55:20.450
processed, one of the fantastic things about
an elastic environment is you don't have to

00:55:20.450 --> 00:55:26.599
attempt to provision in advance and pay for
in advance all of that spike.

00:55:26.599 --> 00:55:31.150
And if you architect your stuff correctly,
you can scale that out instead of having to

00:55:31.150 --> 00:55:32.609
scale it up.

00:55:32.609 --> 00:55:37.690
And we were really -- we were really nervous,
not because we weren't prepared.

00:55:37.690 --> 00:55:42.530
The team that worked on this does fantastic
prep, and you guys were a great partner.

00:55:42.530 --> 00:55:43.530
Diane Greene: Do have great technologists.

00:55:43.530 --> 00:55:44.569
Paul Gaffney: I mean, outstanding prep.

00:55:44.569 --> 00:55:46.730
But everyone is kind of nervous.

00:55:46.730 --> 00:55:51.900
Thankfully our 400,000 folks do such a nice
job each year that more folks do business

00:55:51.900 --> 00:55:53.190
with us each year.

00:55:53.190 --> 00:55:59.770
So this last Black Friday was an all-time
high for us, and we were running meaningful

00:55:59.770 --> 00:56:03.180
portions of our eCommerce infrastructure on
Google infrastructure on Google Cloud for

00:56:03.180 --> 00:56:04.830
the first time.

00:56:04.830 --> 00:56:07.140
And it was our smoothest performance ever.

00:56:07.140 --> 00:56:08.950
Diane Greene: Just tremendous to see.

00:56:08.950 --> 00:56:13.550
And it's great to see you using Kubernetes
and containers.

00:56:13.550 --> 00:56:15.170
How about your work with big data?

00:56:15.170 --> 00:56:21.220
Paul Gaffney: I think, as you know, a very
interesting off-shoot of our partnership on

00:56:21.220 --> 00:56:27.600
cloud was thinking about, okay, we're going
to be moving some main line, line of business

00:56:27.600 --> 00:56:31.530
workload from on-premises to the cloud.

00:56:31.530 --> 00:56:33.840
Should we be doing the same thing with data?

00:56:33.840 --> 00:56:37.799
And as the audience might imagine, we've got
a lot of data.

00:56:37.799 --> 00:56:42.210
And we replenish those 2,000 stores continuously.

00:56:42.210 --> 00:56:47.730
And we have started to move quite a bit of
our data analytics environment from proprietary

00:56:47.730 --> 00:56:52.930
on-premises infrastructure onto BigQuery and
other parts.

00:56:52.930 --> 00:56:56.619
I think, you know, I'm excited by the technology.

00:56:56.619 --> 00:57:01.680
But, frankly, I'm more excited by the investments
you guys are making in talent.

00:57:01.680 --> 00:57:07.539
And I'm looking forward to the work we're
going to do on exploring some really interesting

00:57:07.539 --> 00:57:12.780
problems where we think that the machines
can help us understand some things perhaps

00:57:12.780 --> 00:57:15.230
that the humans find difficult to understand.

00:57:15.230 --> 00:57:16.230
Diane Greene: Yeah.

00:57:16.230 --> 00:57:18.660
I mean, we're really looking forward to this
ongoing work.

00:57:18.660 --> 00:57:23.900
And I can tell you that Fei Fei Li and her
cloud machine learning group are really excited

00:57:23.900 --> 00:57:26.960
about what they're going to be able to do
with Home Depot.

00:57:26.960 --> 00:57:28.440
Thanks so much, Paul, for being here.

00:57:28.440 --> 00:57:29.440
Paul Gaffney: Great pleasure, Diane.

00:57:29.440 --> 00:57:30.440
Thank you.

00:57:30.440 --> 00:57:32.559
Diane Greene: Okay.

00:57:32.559 --> 00:57:34.210
Bye-bye.

00:57:34.210 --> 00:57:35.869
Thanks.

00:57:35.869 --> 00:57:39.390
[ Applause ]
So now we're going to turn and we're going

00:57:39.390 --> 00:57:46.670
to talk about security, privacy data analytics
that scale with a big bank.

00:57:46.670 --> 00:57:52.960
And here to lead that discussion and tell
you about it, I'm delighted to welcome Tariq

00:57:52.960 --> 00:57:58.350
Shaukat, Google Cloud's President for all
customer-facing operations.

00:57:58.350 --> 00:58:06.570
[ Applause ]
Tariq Shaukat: Thank you, Diane.

00:58:06.570 --> 00:58:10.680
Good morning, everybody.

00:58:10.680 --> 00:58:15.770
So you've heard a lot today from our customers,
and you've heard a lot today from Diane and

00:58:15.770 --> 00:58:20.390
from Sundar about what our mission is here
at Google Cloud.

00:58:20.390 --> 00:58:25.799
But to put it in the terms of what we're looking
to do for customers, we very much view our

00:58:25.799 --> 00:58:33.880
mission at Google Cloud and on our customer
team as generating real -- a real step change

00:58:33.880 --> 00:58:36.420
in business value for our customers.

00:58:36.420 --> 00:58:41.980
That business value happens in a number of
different ways and increasingly in mission-critical

00:58:41.980 --> 00:58:42.980
applications.

00:58:42.980 --> 00:58:48.089
It happens, first of all, at the cost-savings
level of how do you actually move to a more

00:58:48.089 --> 00:58:53.050
flexible environment, how do you move to a
more agile environment, how do you move to

00:58:53.050 --> 00:58:54.390
a lower cost environment.

00:58:54.390 --> 00:59:01.040
And so that is absolutely one of the major
conversations that we have with our customers.

00:59:01.040 --> 00:59:06.619
As you heard Paul talk about and you heard
Mike from Disney talk about, we also are increasingly

00:59:06.619 --> 00:59:13.230
seeing the cloud being used as a platform
to drive real tangible revenue growth for

00:59:13.230 --> 00:59:18.809
companies, for them to both create new applications
but also critically importantly to understand

00:59:18.809 --> 00:59:22.589
their customers better, to get into their
data better, and to really understand how

00:59:22.589 --> 00:59:28.260
to become closer to their customers, how to
become more efficient in their operations,

00:59:28.260 --> 00:59:30.390
and how to engage their employees better.

00:59:30.390 --> 00:59:34.960
So you have the cost-savings piece, and you
have the revenue-generation piece.

00:59:34.960 --> 00:59:41.910
But the third part that really has been a
thrilling evolution of the market is how many

00:59:41.910 --> 00:59:46.529
companies are really coming to Google Cloud
now and saying, "We need a partnership."

00:59:46.529 --> 00:59:51.529
And we need a partnership to help transform
our business, to really help us think about

00:59:51.529 --> 00:59:56.400
what is the business model of the future and
how do we take our existing operations and

00:59:56.400 --> 01:00:03.980
how do we move that in a both gradual way
and in a step-changed way into the future.

01:00:03.980 --> 01:00:10.619
One of the most thrilling partnerships that
we have in this regard is with HSBC.

01:00:10.619 --> 01:00:14.690
HSBC challenges us in each and every one of
these areas, on the cost savings, on the revenue

01:00:14.690 --> 01:00:18.450
growth, and on the transformation levels every
day.

01:00:18.450 --> 01:00:23.400
Now, you may know them as one of the largest
financial institutions in the world.

01:00:23.400 --> 01:00:31.210
They have over 150-year history, 4,000 offices
in 70 countries, and over 2 trillion, $2 trillion

01:00:31.210 --> 01:00:33.529
of assets on their balance sheet.

01:00:33.529 --> 01:00:38.020
Now, to tell you more about this, I'm truly
delighted to welcome to the Google Cloud stage

01:00:38.020 --> 01:00:42.109
Darryl West who is the global chief information
officer at HSBC.

01:00:42.109 --> 01:00:47.220
And he's going to share his thinking about
the roll of cloud and cloud scale data analytics

01:00:47.220 --> 01:00:51.340
and machine learning for the financial services
world.

01:00:51.340 --> 01:00:52.960
Darryl?

01:00:52.960 --> 01:00:57.799
[ Video ]
Speaker: We are living in a type of rapid

01:00:57.799 --> 01:01:05.710
digital change, where the only limits are
the scale of our ideas and degree of identification.

01:01:05.710 --> 01:01:10.319
The technology ideas of today will be the
intuitive habits of tomorrow.

01:01:10.319 --> 01:01:18.839
Never before has innovation promised so much
touching so many in so many different ways.

01:01:18.839 --> 01:01:21.829
Our frontier is financial technology.

01:01:21.829 --> 01:01:27.730
It's changing the way we work, make money,
and the way we trust each other.

01:01:27.730 --> 01:01:33.260
To succeed, we need to adopt the mindset of
the silicon hubs of the world and create an

01:01:33.260 --> 01:01:39.869
incubator of free thinker, thought leaders,
innovators born of collaborative spirit.

01:01:39.869 --> 01:01:45.529
All fueled by a belief that technology will
enhance our customers' financial lives to

01:01:45.529 --> 01:01:51.089
imagine a future beyond what we already know,
creating a team with the power to transform

01:01:51.089 --> 01:01:54.640
the world's leading financial enterprise.

01:01:54.640 --> 01:01:57.780
We are technology.

01:01:57.780 --> 01:02:05.789
[ Applause ]
Darryl West: Thank you, Tariq.

01:02:05.789 --> 01:02:07.190
Good morning, everybody.

01:02:07.190 --> 01:02:12.369
It gives me immense pleasure to be able to
be here with you today, particularly on International

01:02:12.369 --> 01:02:14.619
Women's Day.

01:02:14.619 --> 01:02:20.730
I've been at HSBC now for a couple of years,
and I have the honor and privilege of leading

01:02:20.730 --> 01:02:21.730
an awesome I.T.

01:02:21.730 --> 01:02:27.430
team that's at the center of an amazing transformation
happening in our organization.

01:02:27.430 --> 01:02:32.869
I'm going to talk about our commitment to
cloud, and I'm going to talk about how we're

01:02:32.869 --> 01:02:40.400
collaborating and partnering with Google to
make HSBC a simpler, better, and faster organization,

01:02:40.400 --> 01:02:46.080
an organization focused on amazing customer
experiences through digital channels and a

01:02:46.080 --> 01:02:51.019
company committed to using the latest technology
in data analytics and machine learning to

01:02:51.019 --> 01:02:52.950
transform the way we run our business.

01:02:52.950 --> 01:02:57.610
Now, I'm sure many of you in the audience
are travelers and I'm sure many of you have

01:02:57.610 --> 01:03:03.120
stood in airport walkways and seen the HSBC
advertisements, and they're quite distinctive.

01:03:03.120 --> 01:03:07.799
And you've probably asked the question, "Who
are these people?"

01:03:07.799 --> 01:03:09.920
Well, who are we?

01:03:09.920 --> 01:03:12.170
We're the world's largest international bank.

01:03:12.170 --> 01:03:15.560
As Tariq said, we've been around for 150 years.

01:03:15.560 --> 01:03:18.569
We're present in 70 countries.

01:03:18.569 --> 01:03:20.529
We have 37 million customers.

01:03:20.529 --> 01:03:26.960
Customers ranging from individuals to small
businesses, mid-sized corporates, large global

01:03:26.960 --> 01:03:31.940
companies, as well as governments as well.

01:03:31.940 --> 01:03:34.089
So we have a huge business, a huge global
business.

01:03:34.089 --> 01:03:38.630
We're the number one bank in the world for
trade finance and cross-border financing and

01:03:38.630 --> 01:03:42.250
we're a significant player in the global foreign
exchange markets.

01:03:42.250 --> 01:03:46.869
So given our central position in global finance,
as you can imagine, we are a systemically

01:03:46.869 --> 01:03:50.050
important financial institution heavily regulated.

01:03:50.050 --> 01:03:56.090
Now, in the banking business, clearly trust
and confidence is a central part of our business.

01:03:56.090 --> 01:04:01.470
We have to make sure that our customers feel
confident and trust in us to be the custodians

01:04:01.470 --> 01:04:03.099
of their assets.

01:04:03.099 --> 01:04:10.359
So, you know, information security, reliability,
and resilience in how we deliver our services

01:04:10.359 --> 01:04:12.890
are fundamental to our business.

01:04:12.890 --> 01:04:20.799
Now, apart from having the $2.4 trillion of
assets on the balance sheet, we also have

01:04:20.799 --> 01:04:25.450
at the core of the company a massive asset
in our data and, you know, what's been happening

01:04:25.450 --> 01:04:30.109
in the last two or three years is a massive
growth in the size of our data asset.

01:04:30.109 --> 01:04:34.950
So as you can see here, 56 petabytes of data
in 2014.

01:04:34.950 --> 01:04:35.950
That's actually doubled.

01:04:35.950 --> 01:04:39.910
As of now, sitting here in 2017, over 100
petabytes of data.

01:04:39.910 --> 01:04:44.040
And what's happening is our customers are
adopting our digital channels more aggressively.

01:04:44.040 --> 01:04:48.510
We're collecting much more data about how
our customers are interacting with us.

01:04:48.510 --> 01:04:53.609
And obviously embedded in this data is massive
insights, and what we need to do as a bank

01:04:53.609 --> 01:04:58.730
is work with partners to enable us to understand
what's happening with this data, draw out

01:04:58.730 --> 01:05:02.819
the insights so we can run a better business,
and create some amazing customer experiences.

01:05:02.819 --> 01:05:09.950
Now, our journey in data is very similar to
other companies.

01:05:09.950 --> 01:05:14.769
This is no -- this is no different to many
enterprise companies, the history here.

01:05:14.769 --> 01:05:16.799
We have lots of good old-fashioned databases.

01:05:16.799 --> 01:05:23.150
All of our core systems are running on product
systems that have been around for 20, 30,

01:05:23.150 --> 01:05:24.150
even 40 years.

01:05:24.150 --> 01:05:29.410
The systems are robust and they're scalable
and they do a great job but they don't have

01:05:29.410 --> 01:05:34.230
database structures that actually allow us
to really do the data analytics, the machine

01:05:34.230 --> 01:05:35.470
learning that we need to do.

01:05:35.470 --> 01:05:41.360
So the history of enterprises like mine is
that we typically did extracts into traditional

01:05:41.360 --> 01:05:46.960
data warehousing platforms, which worked well
for many years but of recent times they've

01:05:46.960 --> 01:05:50.490
become expensive to run and difficult to use.

01:05:50.490 --> 01:05:56.589
So we took the plunge about three years ago
to really embed ourselves into the evolving

01:05:56.589 --> 01:05:58.800
Hadoop ecosystem.

01:05:58.800 --> 01:06:03.050
And for an organization like mine that has
this traditional history of what we call legacy

01:06:03.050 --> 01:06:07.450
platforms, it's been a tough -- it's been
a tough road for us.

01:06:07.450 --> 01:06:09.400
All of this has been done on-prem.

01:06:09.400 --> 01:06:14.500
We've had to provision large infrastructure,
physical infrastructure, build out data centers,

01:06:14.500 --> 01:06:19.589
and hire talented new people that have understanding
of these new technologies.

01:06:19.589 --> 01:06:23.390
So we thought about this very carefully in
the last year and we said to ourselves, "Well,

01:06:23.390 --> 01:06:24.519
really, we're a bank.

01:06:24.519 --> 01:06:26.240
At the core of our business, we're a bank.

01:06:26.240 --> 01:06:31.390
But we also have a significant technology
company embedded within the organization."

01:06:31.390 --> 01:06:35.440
And the question that I was asking the management
team is, you know, "Do we really want to compete

01:06:35.440 --> 01:06:37.740
with the cloud providers and people like Google?

01:06:37.740 --> 01:06:41.710
Are we really going to try and do what they
do as well as they do it?"

01:06:41.710 --> 01:06:46.869
I think our conclusion was that it was better
for our business if we adopt a cloud-first

01:06:46.869 --> 01:06:48.799
strategy.

01:06:48.799 --> 01:06:53.170
So we started working with Google about six
months ago now, so it's still early in our

01:06:53.170 --> 01:06:58.170
relationship, but we've been working with
them on some of the most important and critical

01:06:58.170 --> 01:07:00.390
business problems that we have to solve in
our business.

01:07:00.390 --> 01:07:05.980
To give you an idea of what these types of
problems are, so the initial use cases here

01:07:05.980 --> 01:07:12.990
are typically characterized by business problems
that have very large data sets and require

01:07:12.990 --> 01:07:16.609
very intense computing capability in short
bursts.

01:07:16.609 --> 01:07:21.710
Now, the first one on this list is all about
anti-money laundering, so as one of our obligations

01:07:21.710 --> 01:07:26.170
of being a bank, we work with the -- with
the governments and the crime agencies to

01:07:26.170 --> 01:07:31.069
identify nefarious activity, money laundering
criminal activity.

01:07:31.069 --> 01:07:37.900
Now, we have a set of applications that monitor
a huge time series of data, so a massive data

01:07:37.900 --> 01:07:40.460
set, billions of transactions, for all of
our customers.

01:07:40.460 --> 01:07:46.930
We're running analytics over this huge data
set with great, you know, compute capability

01:07:46.930 --> 01:07:52.769
to identify patterns in the data and to bring
out what looks like nefarious activity within

01:07:52.769 --> 01:07:53.829
our customer base.

01:07:53.829 --> 01:07:58.170
And those patterns that we identify then are
escalated into the agency, so we work with

01:07:58.170 --> 01:08:00.410
them to track down the bad guys.

01:08:00.410 --> 01:08:07.140
So this is an application that clearly requires
massive data sets, great computing capability,

01:08:07.140 --> 01:08:11.619
but also a machine learning capability to
be able to identify the patterns in this huge

01:08:11.619 --> 01:08:14.460
-- this huge data set.

01:08:14.460 --> 01:08:18.401
The other applications that are mentioned
here in terms of finance and risk, we have

01:08:18.401 --> 01:08:19.409
billions of transactions.

01:08:19.409 --> 01:08:23.850
Those transactions need to be aggregated so
we can manage our finances and our risk at

01:08:23.850 --> 01:08:25.400
a global level.

01:08:25.400 --> 01:08:31.150
Our valuation services and our trading business,
as I said, we're a major player in global

01:08:31.150 --> 01:08:32.380
financial markets.

01:08:32.380 --> 01:08:37.009
We need to be able to run complex multicolor
simulations on a regular basis to be able

01:08:37.009 --> 01:08:41.290
to better understand our trading positions
and our risks, so this requires a significant

01:08:41.290 --> 01:08:42.500
compute capability.

01:08:42.500 --> 01:08:47.009
So we were faced with the question of do we
build out a new data center and put, you know,

01:08:47.009 --> 01:08:51.069
thousands of servers and thousands of cores
out there to do this activity or should we

01:08:51.069 --> 01:08:53.760
actually work with somebody who does it as
a core business.

01:08:53.760 --> 01:08:58.049
And our conclusion was rather than build it
out ourselves, we should be working with,

01:08:58.049 --> 01:09:03.000
you know, world-class leaders in this space
and, hence, our work with Google.

01:09:03.000 --> 01:09:08.630
Now, all of this doesn't happen by accident
and there are some critical enablers that

01:09:08.630 --> 01:09:14.040
have to all be in place to make this work
for organizations like us.

01:09:14.040 --> 01:09:18.180
The first couple of boxes here talk about
risk and compliance and information security.

01:09:18.180 --> 01:09:20.670
You know, we are a heavily regulated business.

01:09:20.670 --> 01:09:26.380
We have to provide very resilient, very safe,
and very performant services to our customers,

01:09:26.380 --> 01:09:30.230
and also very agile, because the requirements
of our customers are changing tremendously

01:09:30.230 --> 01:09:31.230
quickly.

01:09:31.230 --> 01:09:36.740
We also have to respect the data protection
and data residency rules that we have.

01:09:36.740 --> 01:09:40.330
In our 70 countries, there are lots of rules
about what data can sit where and what data

01:09:40.330 --> 01:09:45.120
can be share, so we have to respect all of
that and we're working closely with our partner

01:09:45.120 --> 01:09:49.029
at Google on these -- on this construct.

01:09:49.029 --> 01:09:54.130
Information security, as you heard already
this morning, it's a massive issue for everyone.

01:09:54.130 --> 01:09:55.270
Particularly for a bank.

01:09:55.270 --> 01:10:00.230
We have to keep our data safe from the cybercriminals,
and we're working very well with Google on

01:10:00.230 --> 01:10:02.210
solutions in the security space.

01:10:02.210 --> 01:10:07.040
Probably the biggest impact, though, or the
biggest enabler for this journey is the -- are

01:10:07.040 --> 01:10:08.720
the two boxes in the middle here.

01:10:08.720 --> 01:10:13.239
You have to drive a cultural shift in your
business to make this really work.

01:10:13.239 --> 01:10:14.980
You have to adopt an agile methodology.

01:10:14.980 --> 01:10:20.739
You have to adopt a devops mind-set, and you
need to be able to recruit and retain talented

01:10:20.739 --> 01:10:25.820
people that understand how to use these new
technologies and work in this way.

01:10:25.820 --> 01:10:27.600
So just a point of note.

01:10:27.600 --> 01:10:28.600
We are recruiting.

01:10:28.600 --> 01:10:29.600
Okay.

01:10:29.600 --> 01:10:32.670
[ Laughter ]
Darryl West: And we're a great company, just

01:10:32.670 --> 01:10:33.670
like Google.

01:10:33.670 --> 01:10:37.170
[ Laughter ]
Darryl West: So the other issue here on the

01:10:37.170 --> 01:10:38.739
second box here is about integration.

01:10:38.739 --> 01:10:41.000
I mentioned the legacy platforms we have.

01:10:41.000 --> 01:10:43.320
All of our core data is sitting on these systems
of record.

01:10:43.320 --> 01:10:48.360
We have to make sure that we can integrate
all of this technology complexity to work

01:10:48.360 --> 01:10:52.380
with the Google platform, the Google Cloud
Platform, so this integration layer is very,

01:10:52.380 --> 01:10:53.440
very important.

01:10:53.440 --> 01:10:56.690
You've got to get that culture in place, you
have to get the people that understand how

01:10:56.690 --> 01:11:00.760
to do it, and you have to build the integration
layer so you'll be able to it all work in

01:11:00.760 --> 01:11:02.050
that rapid cycle.

01:11:02.050 --> 01:11:05.050
And lastly, of course, data preparation.

01:11:05.050 --> 01:11:08.800
You know, we have, like most big enterprises,
a data sprawl.

01:11:08.800 --> 01:11:11.590
As I said, we've had a massive growth of data.

01:11:11.590 --> 01:11:14.800
But also, you need to invest your time and
effort in your data architecture and making

01:11:14.800 --> 01:11:19.739
sure you understand where your crown jewels
are, where the copies are, and get a really

01:11:19.739 --> 01:11:25.530
good understanding of the preparation of getting
the data ready to be able to interface effectively

01:11:25.530 --> 01:11:27.260
into the cloud partner.

01:11:27.260 --> 01:11:31.310
And obviously agreements of legal and commercial
are obviously very important as well.

01:11:31.310 --> 01:11:39.770
So in summary, these enablers are fundamental
to us achieving our goals at HSBC and we are

01:11:39.770 --> 01:11:46.250
working in very collaborative and partnership
mode with Google to get these enablers in

01:11:46.250 --> 01:11:48.880
place to run a better business.

01:11:48.880 --> 01:11:57.920
[ Applause ]
Tariq Shaukat: Thank you, Darryl.

01:11:57.920 --> 01:12:03.250
That's a fascinating journey that you guys
are on there at HSBC.

01:12:03.250 --> 01:12:05.670
Just a couple of questions for you.

01:12:05.670 --> 01:12:10.970
You'd mentioned that you did a pretty thorough
investigation of the technologies, particularly

01:12:10.970 --> 01:12:15.199
as you were looking at us, around DataFlow
and BigQuery.

01:12:15.199 --> 01:12:17.500
What aspects convinced you to move forward
with GCP?

01:12:17.500 --> 01:12:18.550
Darryl West: Yeah.

01:12:18.550 --> 01:12:23.630
We've -- we spent a good amount of time working
with all the big cloud providers and evaluating

01:12:23.630 --> 01:12:24.630
their products.

01:12:24.630 --> 01:12:31.290
I think if you look at the scarce talent we
have on data analysts and data scientists,

01:12:31.290 --> 01:12:35.659
they need tools that allow them to do their
work in a very productive way, so they've

01:12:35.659 --> 01:12:39.969
-- we've found as we've worked with your team
and your subject matter experts working with

01:12:39.969 --> 01:12:45.940
our data scientists, that your suite of products
have been very performant, very easy to use.

01:12:45.940 --> 01:12:49.929
You know, there's always a challenge of getting
data scientists to shift from their favorite

01:12:49.929 --> 01:12:54.560
platform to a new one and I think the way
that your products are actually being configured

01:12:54.560 --> 01:12:57.810
and presented to our scientists, they've really
enjoyed that experience.

01:12:57.810 --> 01:13:00.050
So, you know, it's early in our relationship.

01:13:00.050 --> 01:13:04.250
We're still in pilot mode and we hope to get
these use cases to production in the coming

01:13:04.250 --> 01:13:08.250
weeks and months, but I think our experience
so far has been very positive.

01:13:08.250 --> 01:13:09.949
Tariq Shaukat: Terrific.

01:13:09.949 --> 01:13:13.880
What is -- just looking forward the next three
to five years, how do you see the cloud really

01:13:13.880 --> 01:13:16.449
playing out and all these technologies impacting
your business?

01:13:16.449 --> 01:13:17.449
Darryl West: Yeah.

01:13:17.449 --> 01:13:21.630
Look, it's very difficult to be able to predict
the future, even three years ahead.

01:13:21.630 --> 01:13:22.630
That's a long way away, right?

01:13:22.630 --> 01:13:23.730
So the world's going to change.

01:13:23.730 --> 01:13:29.040
I think what's definitely going to be consistent
is that our data is going to continue to grow.

01:13:29.040 --> 01:13:32.960
Massive data sets need to be managed.

01:13:32.960 --> 01:13:35.190
We know also we're going to be pushed to go
faster.

01:13:35.190 --> 01:13:39.949
I mean, our customer base is dragging us into
a new world and consuming, you know, services

01:13:39.949 --> 01:13:45.270
on new digital channels which will require
us to be agile and fast to market.

01:13:45.270 --> 01:13:50.150
We know we're going to have the need to use
data analytics, and particularly machine learning,

01:13:50.150 --> 01:13:51.719
to run a better business.

01:13:51.719 --> 01:13:55.310
So it's going to get bigger, it's going to
get more complex, and we're going to need

01:13:55.310 --> 01:14:00.000
to partner with people like Google and others
to be able to use the best technology in the

01:14:00.000 --> 01:14:01.820
world to solve these problems for our business.

01:14:01.820 --> 01:14:03.360
Tariq Shaukat: Great.

01:14:03.360 --> 01:14:07.350
Well, finally, I think everyone in the audience,
I'm sure, could use a little advice.

01:14:07.350 --> 01:14:10.790
You guys are leaning very forward into the
cloud and cloud technology.

01:14:10.790 --> 01:14:14.380
What advice would you give everyone here for
how to really start their journey?

01:14:14.380 --> 01:14:15.380
Darryl West: Yeah.

01:14:15.380 --> 01:14:19.310
Well, I think the first thing you need to
do is you have to make the jump in thinking

01:14:19.310 --> 01:14:21.560
to make yourself a cloud-first company.

01:14:21.560 --> 01:14:27.080
It requires, you know, getting everybody on
board in the technology team but also the

01:14:27.080 --> 01:14:28.080
business.

01:14:28.080 --> 01:14:30.441
You've got to get the business to think about
things in a very different way.

01:14:30.441 --> 01:14:35.010
And I mentioned the criticality of new ways
of working with agile methods, devops methods

01:14:35.010 --> 01:14:36.010
is really important.

01:14:36.010 --> 01:14:40.650
But the first thing is, make that jump and
be bold and have courage to go and do the

01:14:40.650 --> 01:14:43.080
innovative things.

01:14:43.080 --> 01:14:46.949
Second thing is, pick a partner that has the
same type of culture as you.

01:14:46.949 --> 01:14:49.889
I think you need to -- if you're going to
be innovative, you're going to be bold and

01:14:49.889 --> 01:14:53.880
take on big challenges, you need a partner
that's going to think the same way and work

01:14:53.880 --> 01:14:56.679
in the same ways of working.

01:14:56.679 --> 01:15:02.700
And just I think basically the conclusion
I've come to is have a good look out there,

01:15:02.700 --> 01:15:07.330
meet, you know, the people in the market,
because they're all very different, and certainly

01:15:07.330 --> 01:15:10.780
from my perspective the chemistry between
the people and the teams is actually the number

01:15:10.780 --> 01:15:14.860
one -- the number one thing, because what
we're doing is complex and what we're doing

01:15:14.860 --> 01:15:19.330
is difficult in many cases, and the chemistry
and the culture of the organizations is very

01:15:19.330 --> 01:15:21.030
important and that's --
Tariq Shaukat: And it's a multiyear journey.

01:15:21.030 --> 01:15:24.130
Darryl West: And it's a multiyear journey
so you have to like who you're hanging out

01:15:24.130 --> 01:15:26.290
with for the years ahead.

01:15:26.290 --> 01:15:27.290
Tariq Shaukat: Exactly.

01:15:27.290 --> 01:15:30.250
Well, again, on behalf of all of Google Cloud,
we're really thrilled that you're here today,

01:15:30.250 --> 01:15:31.250
so thank you very much.

01:15:31.250 --> 01:15:32.250
Darryl West: Thank you.

01:15:32.250 --> 01:15:38.209
Tariq Shaukat: And I'd like to welcome Diane
Greene back onto the stage.

01:15:38.209 --> 01:15:46.460
[ Applause ]
Diane Greene: Thank you, Tariq and Darryl.

01:15:46.460 --> 01:15:47.670
That was fantastic.

01:15:47.670 --> 01:15:54.530
And now, we're going to have a look at how
an iconic eCommerce company, eBay, is thinking

01:15:54.530 --> 01:15:58.000
about the cloud.

01:15:58.000 --> 01:16:03.620
I think we have a video first, and I want
to welcome on stage RJ Pittman, who is head

01:16:03.620 --> 01:16:06.960
of products, CTO --
Speaker: eBay is all over the world.

01:16:06.960 --> 01:16:12.230
We transfer about 30 gigs a second and search
through 1 billion active listings in our quest

01:16:12.230 --> 01:16:13.660
to find your "perfect."

01:16:13.660 --> 01:16:20.159
Through cutting-edge technology and the performance
and scale of cloud, we strive to meet our

01:16:20.159 --> 01:16:25.530
customers where they are and transform the
future of commerce to help find your version

01:16:25.530 --> 01:16:29.670
of "perfect."

01:16:29.670 --> 01:16:31.940
The future of commerce from eBay.

01:16:31.940 --> 01:16:33.639
Diane Greene: Cool.

01:16:33.639 --> 01:16:37.040
[ Applause ]
Diane Greene: RJ, thank you for coming.

01:16:37.040 --> 01:16:38.040
Great to have you here.

01:16:38.040 --> 01:16:40.090
RJ Pittman: Great to see you.

01:16:40.090 --> 01:16:45.720
Diane Greene: So RJ, you've had a phenomenal
amount of experience working in online environments.

01:16:45.720 --> 01:16:49.790
Why don't you start talking about how your
view of the cloud has changed.

01:16:49.790 --> 01:16:54.390
RJ Pittman: Well, the biggest thing is, it's
moving and it's moving fast.

01:16:54.390 --> 01:17:02.130
In the last four years, it's, from my perspective,
gone from a very powerful and compelling proposition

01:17:02.130 --> 01:17:04.270
for outsourcing your I.T.

01:17:04.270 --> 01:17:09.570
infrastructure and shifting some things so
that you can focus on your core business to

01:17:09.570 --> 01:17:18.010
now we see it as a strategic growth engine
and a strategic growth engine that accelerates

01:17:18.010 --> 01:17:19.010
innovation.

01:17:19.010 --> 01:17:23.739
And this is a very different position that
the cloud has taken and it's playing a very

01:17:23.739 --> 01:17:26.310
important role for us in that manner at eBay.

01:17:26.310 --> 01:17:29.770
Diane Greene: And what do you think that means
for eBay?

01:17:29.770 --> 01:17:34.600
RJ Pittman: Well, you know, so in the video,
as you can see, I mean, we've been around

01:17:34.600 --> 01:17:36.510
for a long time.

01:17:36.510 --> 01:17:38.590
The business is continuing to grow.

01:17:38.590 --> 01:17:45.090
We have over 1 billion live listings at any
given moment in 200 countries around the world

01:17:45.090 --> 01:17:46.870
--
Diane Greene: Yeah.

01:17:46.870 --> 01:17:54.570
RJ Pittman: -- 167 million shoppers, and so
you can imagine that if we start moving the

01:17:54.570 --> 01:18:00.590
capabilities of the cloud to eBay, we're not
just changing our infrastructure and changing

01:18:00.590 --> 01:18:01.590
our business.

01:18:01.590 --> 01:18:06.489
We're changing the experience for all of those
shoppers and all of our buyers and sellers

01:18:06.489 --> 01:18:07.920
around the world.

01:18:07.920 --> 01:18:10.720
And that's the really important connective
tissue here.

01:18:10.720 --> 01:18:13.510
This is not just a technology exercise for
us.

01:18:13.510 --> 01:18:17.300
It is absolutely a customer-centric effort.

01:18:17.300 --> 01:18:19.080
Diane Greene: Yeah.

01:18:19.080 --> 01:18:20.080
Absolutely.

01:18:20.080 --> 01:18:24.150
And, you know, with that kind of phenomenal
growth, I can see that you really are paying

01:18:24.150 --> 01:18:26.890
attention to technology to help you through
it.

01:18:26.890 --> 01:18:27.890
Yeah.

01:18:27.890 --> 01:18:28.890
RJ Pittman: Yeah.

01:18:28.890 --> 01:18:37.320
It's -- you know, for us, the eCommerce space
is competitive and it's moving fast and the

01:18:37.320 --> 01:18:43.840
demands of customers and shoppers today is
changing so quickly, and as new generations

01:18:43.840 --> 01:18:50.520
of buyers come into the world, first mentales,
now Gen Zers, they have very different shopping

01:18:50.520 --> 01:18:54.990
habits, very different expectations, and they're
much higher expectations.

01:18:54.990 --> 01:19:01.230
And so we, eBay, at our scale, if we're, you
know, intent on setting ourselves up to meet

01:19:01.230 --> 01:19:07.020
the demands and exceed the demands of this
next generation of online shoppers, we have

01:19:07.020 --> 01:19:10.710
to move quickly and the cloud has become a
very strategic partner for us in that.

01:19:10.710 --> 01:19:15.020
Diane Greene: So RJ, before we get into our
cool demo, can you just tell me a little bit

01:19:15.020 --> 01:19:17.280
about what you're doing on Google Cloud?

01:19:17.280 --> 01:19:18.810
RJ Pittman: Sure.

01:19:18.810 --> 01:19:24.060
So this was an undertaking for us that we've
been thinking about for a few years, but we

01:19:24.060 --> 01:19:32.510
really got serious about it a year ago, and
we knew that there was a lot of untapped potential

01:19:32.510 --> 01:19:35.440
inside the eBay marketplace and we wanted
to get at it.

01:19:35.440 --> 01:19:40.489
And as a marketplace, let's also remember
that first and foremost we're a tech company

01:19:40.489 --> 01:19:45.219
and we utilize technology to differentiate,
to compete, to innovate, and to create these

01:19:45.219 --> 01:19:49.840
amazing customer experiences for all of our
customers around the world.

01:19:49.840 --> 01:19:56.190
And so for us, we went out on a mission and
said, "If we could bring the eBay marketplace

01:19:56.190 --> 01:20:01.219
to a modern stack environment, if we could
bring our customers to a place with we can

01:20:01.219 --> 01:20:07.179
be innovating almost every day and bringing
great new features and capabilities to them,

01:20:07.179 --> 01:20:11.199
you know, that would give us a tremendous
advantage and a ton of momentum."

01:20:11.199 --> 01:20:19.150
So literally in the space of about five months,
we took a great team of engineers and set

01:20:19.150 --> 01:20:24.860
off to do exactly that, and in five months
we went from nothing to the entire 1 billion

01:20:24.860 --> 01:20:28.840
live listings of eBay running in GCP.

01:20:28.840 --> 01:20:30.969
It was not a test.

01:20:30.969 --> 01:20:33.110
It was not a prototype.

01:20:33.110 --> 01:20:40.760
It quickly moved to a production-grade environment
for us that we ended up launching and bringing

01:20:40.760 --> 01:20:47.510
into market six months ahead of our plan,
and mind you, that in total, start to finish,

01:20:47.510 --> 01:20:49.400
was about seven months.

01:20:49.400 --> 01:20:51.230
So it can be done.

01:20:51.230 --> 01:20:52.230
Diane Greene: Yes.

01:20:52.230 --> 01:20:53.880
RJ Pittman: Do not fear the cloud.

01:20:53.880 --> 01:20:54.880
It can be done.

01:20:54.880 --> 01:20:58.639
And look, in our business, this is super important.

01:20:58.639 --> 01:21:04.150
You can't -- you have to be careful when you're
serving live customers in a live marketplace

01:21:04.150 --> 01:21:05.150
like that.

01:21:05.150 --> 01:21:09.290
And now the way we -- we've approached it,
we were smart about this and we didn't just

01:21:09.290 --> 01:21:16.400
flip the switch and, you know, move the entire
$85 billion marketplace business at the throw

01:21:16.400 --> 01:21:17.400
of a switch.

01:21:17.400 --> 01:21:21.949
We're actually running a parallel swim lane
and that gives us lots of latitude to play

01:21:21.949 --> 01:21:22.949
with.

01:21:22.949 --> 01:21:23.949
And they're both live systems.

01:21:23.949 --> 01:21:30.250
And this is very difficult to do in any -- in
most environments because we're transacting,

01:21:30.250 --> 01:21:37.969
as you saw, thousands of orders a second,
and we now have two hot/hot systems running.

01:21:37.969 --> 01:21:44.150
Our core system on-prem and then we have the
new Google platform both serving customers.

01:21:44.150 --> 01:21:48.860
And we've had to build a system that ensured
that we didn't create two separate marketplaces.

01:21:48.860 --> 01:21:54.290
Meaning no two buyers could buy the same one
item that's for sale because that would create,

01:21:54.290 --> 01:21:56.719
obviously, a lot of problems for us.

01:21:56.719 --> 01:22:01.429
And it was actually one of the most important
decision factors for us in really leaning

01:22:01.429 --> 01:22:07.239
in on the Google platform is the services
and the capability that Google Offers that

01:22:07.239 --> 01:22:12.889
nobody else did that allowed us to build a
true replication -- replicated system that

01:22:12.889 --> 01:22:20.199
runs in real time side by side to help companies
of our size and scale and complexity make

01:22:20.199 --> 01:22:23.300
that transition much more seamlessly to the
cloud.

01:22:23.300 --> 01:22:29.631
Now, mind you, the other cool thing about
it is it didn't just allow us to go to a cloud

01:22:29.631 --> 01:22:36.750
instance here in North America, but using
the global footprint of GCP, we took the show

01:22:36.750 --> 01:22:43.710
on the road and we were able to test our theory
and look at lighting up the Google Cloud in

01:22:43.710 --> 01:22:49.380
multiple countries around the world, so that
we could be serving where our customers are

01:22:49.380 --> 01:22:54.050
and providing a great high-speed performance
as if that data center was sitting in their

01:22:54.050 --> 01:22:55.050
backyard.

01:22:55.050 --> 01:22:59.020
We've not been able to offer that kind of
experience and that kind of performance in

01:22:59.020 --> 01:23:05.320
the 22 years, you know, that we've been around,
so this is really breakthrough for us, and

01:23:05.320 --> 01:23:08.880
it's our customers that are the ones that
really stand to gain.

01:23:08.880 --> 01:23:12.650
Diane Greene: That's just a great story of
what you have done.

01:23:12.650 --> 01:23:20.140
I think in another area you have leveraged
AI for your catalog.

01:23:20.140 --> 01:23:21.200
What about that?

01:23:21.200 --> 01:23:27.080
RJ Pitman: This is a really cool part of the
story where we have a billion live listings,

01:23:27.080 --> 01:23:30.580
and we have 22 years of historical transaction
data.

01:23:30.580 --> 01:23:35.600
We have more economic data on the supply and
demand information on virtually any product

01:23:35.600 --> 01:23:39.750
that has been made and sold in the last 20
years.

01:23:39.750 --> 01:23:44.690
We know what it sold for, what the demand
was, what countries, you know, are most interested,

01:23:44.690 --> 01:23:45.871
and what products and when.

01:23:45.871 --> 01:23:51.500
You can imagine if we could then start to
encapsulate and capture all of that knowledge

01:23:51.500 --> 01:23:56.100
and bring it forward for our shoppers, you
could build arguably one of the smartest shopping

01:23:56.100 --> 01:23:59.170
experiences and smartest catalogs on the planet.

01:23:59.170 --> 01:24:04.840
And the challenge there, too, has been getting
to a place where we had truly the computing

01:24:04.840 --> 01:24:10.949
capacity and performance to get our arms around
literally billions and billions of records

01:24:10.949 --> 01:24:15.650
that go back in time but also the new ones
that are created every day that there's transactions

01:24:15.650 --> 01:24:17.429
on eBay.

01:24:17.429 --> 01:24:23.920
And going to GCP, we have the scale and the
performance that allowed us to lay down some

01:24:23.920 --> 01:24:28.220
really great AI technology going well beyond
machine learning and going very seriously

01:24:28.220 --> 01:24:33.330
into deep learning, natural language understanding
and computer vision to create something that

01:24:33.330 --> 01:24:34.670
we called eBay ShopBot.

01:24:34.670 --> 01:24:38.210
And this thing is pretty great.

01:24:38.210 --> 01:24:43.310
And it is the largest marketplace in social
where we took our billion-item catalog and

01:24:43.310 --> 01:24:49.510
merged it with a billion people in the social
graph and brought them a highly personalized

01:24:49.510 --> 01:24:55.760
shopping assistant that gets to know you,
that understands natural language, and can

01:24:55.760 --> 01:25:01.869
start to make really, really great recommendations
across our entire inventory of a billion items

01:25:01.869 --> 01:25:06.550
to give you -- help you find the best item
for you at the right time at the right place

01:25:06.550 --> 01:25:08.300
at the right price.

01:25:08.300 --> 01:25:12.810
And so when technology can start doing the
heavy lifting and provide you with almost

01:25:12.810 --> 01:25:17.980
a concierge-like personalized service, I think
that's when we've really, really made great

01:25:17.980 --> 01:25:18.980
strides.

01:25:18.980 --> 01:25:23.270
That's something that we brought together
with the innovation at eBay and the great

01:25:23.270 --> 01:25:24.280
work going on in Google.

01:25:24.280 --> 01:25:28.600
Diane Greene: I have to say that sounds like
a perfect match for Google Home.

01:25:28.600 --> 01:25:31.440
And we actually have a Google Home sitting
over there.

01:25:31.440 --> 01:25:33.139
Could you show us all what you're talking
about?

01:25:33.139 --> 01:25:38.119
RJ Pitman: In the spirit of going to where
our customers are, we've taken it to Google

01:25:38.119 --> 01:25:40.530
Home because we want to get inside the home.

01:25:40.530 --> 01:25:42.070
Let's do it.

01:25:42.070 --> 01:25:45.540
Diane Greene: Now, you know, we're doing this
live.

01:25:45.540 --> 01:25:47.920
There's, like, 10,000 people on the network.

01:25:47.920 --> 01:25:50.260
We really hope this works.

01:25:50.260 --> 01:25:54.659
[ Laughter ]
RJ Pitman: Hey, Google.

01:25:54.659 --> 01:25:56.030
Let me talk to eBay.

01:25:56.030 --> 01:25:57.850
Google: Sure.

01:25:57.850 --> 01:25:58.850
Here's eBay.

01:25:58.850 --> 01:26:00.580
Speaker: Hi, I'm eBay.

01:26:00.580 --> 01:26:02.480
I'm the world's price guide.

01:26:02.480 --> 01:26:04.610
You can ask me what something is worth.

01:26:04.610 --> 01:26:07.409
RJ Pitman: What's my camera worth?

01:26:07.409 --> 01:26:09.840
Speaker: Is it new or used?

01:26:09.840 --> 01:26:11.820
RJ Pitman: New.

01:26:11.820 --> 01:26:14.380
Speaker: What is the brand of your camera?

01:26:14.380 --> 01:26:16.900
RJ Pitman: Canon.

01:26:16.900 --> 01:26:19.960
Speaker: Tell me the model of your new Canon
camera.

01:26:19.960 --> 01:26:21.520
RJ Pitman: 5D.

01:26:21.520 --> 01:26:26.510
Speaker: What else do you want to tell me
about your Canon camera?

01:26:26.510 --> 01:26:29.420
RJ Pitman: Nothing.

01:26:29.420 --> 01:26:33.030
Speaker: Your camera is worth $2,548.

01:26:33.030 --> 01:26:39.139
RJ Pitman: Not bad.

01:26:39.139 --> 01:26:43.520
[ Applause ]
So this is, you know, maybe an unexpected

01:26:43.520 --> 01:26:48.679
use case but one that's really interesting
and close to the hearts of eBay because we

01:26:48.679 --> 01:26:53.500
support not only our shoppers but also our
tens of millions of sellers all around the

01:26:53.500 --> 01:26:54.690
world.

01:26:54.690 --> 01:26:58.520
And what this allows us to do is start to
bring more sellers into the marketplace.

01:26:58.520 --> 01:27:02.250
You may have lots of really cool things sitting
around your house that you didn't know had

01:27:02.250 --> 01:27:04.010
really great market value.

01:27:04.010 --> 01:27:09.610
Now you will be able to ask Google and ask
eBay through the Google Home partnership to

01:27:09.610 --> 01:27:12.250
get you started and bring you a little bit
closer to eBay.

01:27:12.250 --> 01:27:14.270
And there's so much more to come with this
technology.

01:27:14.270 --> 01:27:15.270
Diane Greene: Absolutely.

01:27:15.270 --> 01:27:16.719
RJ Pitman: Let me tell you this.

01:27:16.719 --> 01:27:21.360
It was five months for us to go from where
we were to in the Google Cloud.

01:27:21.360 --> 01:27:23.890
And it took us five days to get into Google
Home.

01:27:23.890 --> 01:27:26.630
So, you know, with that kind of power, there's
great, great potential.

01:27:26.630 --> 01:27:28.270
[ Applause ]
Diane Greene: RJ, thank you so much.

01:27:28.270 --> 01:27:29.270
RJ Pitman: Thank you.

01:27:29.270 --> 01:27:30.270
Thank you.

01:27:30.270 --> 01:27:31.270
Diane Greene: That was fantastic.

01:27:31.270 --> 01:27:32.270
RJ Pitman: Really fun being here.

01:27:32.270 --> 01:27:33.270
Thanks, everyone.

01:27:33.270 --> 01:27:37.889
Diane Greene: I just love the fat that here's
a chat bot running on Google Cloud talking

01:27:37.889 --> 01:27:39.429
to Google Home.

01:27:39.429 --> 01:27:46.929
We have One Google partnering with eBay.

01:27:46.929 --> 01:27:50.250
And now you're all in for a treat.

01:27:50.250 --> 01:27:52.310
We're going to bring Fei Fei Li on stage.

01:27:52.310 --> 01:27:56.409
I first met Fei Fei when she came to Stanford
back in 2012.

01:27:56.409 --> 01:27:59.120
She's a neighbor of mine.

01:27:59.120 --> 01:28:05.420
And for those of you that don't know, Fei
Fei was really instrumental in the explosion

01:28:05.420 --> 01:28:06.600
of machine learning.

01:28:06.600 --> 01:28:13.260
She built ImageNet, which is the largest database
of labeled images.

01:28:13.260 --> 01:28:20.679
And what that facilitated was all the academic
researchers, industry researchers now had

01:28:20.679 --> 01:28:23.030
something to benchmark against.

01:28:23.030 --> 01:28:29.300
And we started seeing this explosive improvement
and adoption of machine learning.

01:28:29.300 --> 01:28:34.410
So let me just welcome to the stage Fei Fei
Li.

01:28:34.410 --> 01:28:37.290
She is in charge of Google's cloud machine
learning.

01:28:37.290 --> 01:28:45.540
She's had of the AI lab at Stanford and here
now to lead us in our AI efforts at Google.

01:28:45.540 --> 01:28:48.110
Please welcome.

01:28:48.110 --> 01:28:57.110
[ Applause ]
Fei Fei Li: Good morning, everyone.

01:28:57.110 --> 01:29:00.510
My name is Fei Fei Li.

01:29:00.510 --> 01:29:04.500
I'm the chief scientist of Google Cloud AI
and machine learning.

01:29:04.500 --> 01:29:09.620
So in Google's code word, I'm still a Noogler.

01:29:09.620 --> 01:29:15.110
And it's quite an honor and privilege to be
on this stage to share with you some of my

01:29:15.110 --> 01:29:20.800
thoughts about AI, machine learning, and Google
Cloud.

01:29:20.800 --> 01:29:24.820
So the world is changing incredibly fast.

01:29:24.820 --> 01:29:29.220
Some say that we're living in the fourth Industrial
Revolution.

01:29:29.220 --> 01:29:35.950
And many of it is propelled by the phenomenal
force of computing.

01:29:35.950 --> 01:29:43.739
As an AI technologist for nearly 20 years
working on machine learning, computer vision,

01:29:43.739 --> 01:29:53.619
I've witnessed my field growing from a lofty
but academic pursuit to the biggest driver

01:29:53.619 --> 01:29:55.949
of this change.

01:29:55.949 --> 01:30:04.710
But change happens at many scales, and it
takes imagination to see them all.

01:30:04.710 --> 01:30:09.469
Let's take a familiar example, the self-driving
car.

01:30:09.469 --> 01:30:12.040
It's easy to understand the appeal.

01:30:12.040 --> 01:30:20.150
With the help of sensors and algorithms, a
self-driving car reduces accident risks and

01:30:20.150 --> 01:30:26.790
gives us more time to work, socialize, and
just relax while commuting.

01:30:26.790 --> 01:30:29.400
This is great for a single driver.

01:30:29.400 --> 01:30:34.840
But what happens when thousands of people
have self-driving cars?

01:30:34.840 --> 01:30:43.219
Suddenly, through the coordination of vehicles,
traffic congestion is reduced and parking

01:30:43.219 --> 01:30:45.360
is dramatically simplified.

01:30:45.360 --> 01:30:50.210
And what about millions of people having them?

01:30:50.210 --> 01:31:02.100
Entire cities will be reshaped by -- to reflect
the fundamental shift in the use of its infrastructure.

01:31:02.100 --> 01:31:08.480
So the difference between each scale is participation.

01:31:08.480 --> 01:31:16.949
As a technology reaches more people, its impact
becomes more profound.

01:31:16.949 --> 01:31:28.469
This is why the next step for AI must be democratization,
lowering the barriers to entry and making

01:31:28.469 --> 01:31:38.760
it available to the largest possible community
of developers, users, and enterprises.

01:31:38.760 --> 01:31:45.980
Speaking of democratization and reaching many
people, Google Cloud Platform already delivers

01:31:45.980 --> 01:31:52.570
our customers' applications to over a billion
users every day.

01:31:52.570 --> 01:31:54.530
That's a lot of participation.

01:31:54.530 --> 01:32:02.770
Now, if you can only imagine combining the
massive reach of this platform with the power

01:32:02.770 --> 01:32:10.699
of AI, making it available to everyone, then
we stand to witness a greater improvement

01:32:10.699 --> 01:32:19.340
in quality of life than any other time in
history, from finance to education, from manufacturing

01:32:19.340 --> 01:32:23.310
to healthcare, from retail to agriculture.

01:32:23.310 --> 01:32:25.230
You name it.

01:32:25.230 --> 01:32:31.480
This is why delivering AI and machine learning
through Google Cloud excites me.

01:32:31.480 --> 01:32:39.810
It means finally sharing the technology and
insights I've been involved in for years as

01:32:39.810 --> 01:32:43.810
an AI researcher at Stanford.

01:32:43.810 --> 01:32:51.830
That's also where, by the way, I began a collaboration
and partnership in AI with Dr. Jia Li who

01:32:51.830 --> 01:32:55.449
was one of my first Ph.D. students many years
ago.

01:32:55.449 --> 01:33:02.360
I'm very excited that she has joined Google
with me as the head of R&amp;D in AI and machine

01:33:02.360 --> 01:33:04.280
learning at cloud.

01:33:04.280 --> 01:33:12.540
And speaking of International Women's Day
today, this is another bad ass woman in STEM,

01:33:12.540 --> 01:33:16.920
CS, and AI.

01:33:16.920 --> 01:33:25.780
[ Applause ]
There's no shortage of examples of AI solving

01:33:25.780 --> 01:33:33.969
real-world problems, such as the demo we just
saw that eBay -- talking to the eBay ShopBot

01:33:33.969 --> 01:33:36.440
through Google Home.

01:33:36.440 --> 01:33:42.360
As impressive as these achievements are, they're
just the beginning of the transformation of

01:33:42.360 --> 01:33:43.820
the entire enterprise.

01:33:43.820 --> 01:33:52.659
More and more problems are being addressed
by AI, and the tools we use to build AI solutions

01:33:52.659 --> 01:33:58.630
are becoming more and more sophisticated in
their functions but also easier to use.

01:33:58.630 --> 01:34:04.140
This will change our world dramatically, and
it's happening at a faster pace than most

01:34:04.140 --> 01:34:05.830
people think.

01:34:05.830 --> 01:34:08.680
Let's take a look at a few examples.

01:34:08.680 --> 01:34:13.360
AI has been influencing retail as long as
it's existed.

01:34:13.360 --> 01:34:19.230
For instance, machine learning algorithms
are already helping Google's AdSense and shopping

01:34:19.230 --> 01:34:22.889
to deliver relevant information to our customers.

01:34:22.889 --> 01:34:30.659
But so much more is just waiting to be done,
such as supply chain optimization for routes

01:34:30.659 --> 01:34:37.560
and inventory or predicting changes in demand
over time, drone navigation and self-driving

01:34:37.560 --> 01:34:45.349
vehicles for delivery of items customers ordered,
intelligent analysis for loss prevention and

01:34:45.349 --> 01:34:52.010
safety, or understanding customer movements
and perception in stores, to optimize shelf

01:34:52.010 --> 01:34:56.890
space, displays, and visibility.

01:34:56.890 --> 01:35:02.790
Another example for media and culture already
being influenced by AI.

01:35:02.790 --> 01:35:05.290
Do you have a teenager at home?

01:35:05.290 --> 01:35:10.820
Wonder why -- what's the technology that's
mesmerizing them with the cat-ear and rainbow

01:35:10.820 --> 01:35:13.920
filters of the Snapchat app?

01:35:13.920 --> 01:35:17.719
That's a clever computer vision technology.

01:35:17.719 --> 01:35:24.590
Machine learning already delivers Google Photo
automatic image tagging and YouTube's recommendation

01:35:24.590 --> 01:35:25.590
list.

01:35:25.590 --> 01:35:30.190
But media experiences will soon make much
more use of it.

01:35:30.190 --> 01:35:37.470
AR and VR will rely on computer vision for
motion tracking, environment detection, and

01:35:37.470 --> 01:35:39.020
games.

01:35:39.020 --> 01:35:46.159
More and more news content will be automatically
generated allowing journalists to focus on

01:35:46.159 --> 01:35:48.200
bigger and deeper stories.

01:35:48.200 --> 01:35:56.659
And AI will play a growing role in helping
us to create and stylize our own content such

01:35:56.659 --> 01:36:01.830
as videos, music, and artwork.

01:36:01.830 --> 01:36:07.860
In the financial services world, we're already
seeing machine learning to help fairly and

01:36:07.860 --> 01:36:14.949
intelligently predict credit card risk for
new applicants or even anticipate delinquencies

01:36:14.949 --> 01:36:17.920
among existing customers.

01:36:17.920 --> 01:36:23.420
And many similar advances are in the work
as we speak.

01:36:23.420 --> 01:36:26.760
Insurance claims will be assessed by machine
learning agents.

01:36:26.760 --> 01:36:36.030
Banking will become even more virtual as conversation
bots take over for call centers or even in-person

01:36:36.030 --> 01:36:38.969
bankers when managing finances.

01:36:38.969 --> 01:36:46.489
And as HSBC said earlier, our own perception
will be augmented with intelligent agents

01:36:46.489 --> 01:36:51.210
to flag criminal activity such as money laundering
or fraud.

01:36:51.210 --> 01:36:59.480
Last, but not least, example here, healthcare
is among the most profound applications of

01:36:59.480 --> 01:37:02.880
AI for truly improving people's lives.

01:37:02.880 --> 01:37:08.300
We've already seen some incredible AI achievements
in recent years.

01:37:08.300 --> 01:37:15.360
A few months ago my colleagues at Google Brain
has shown that using a deep-learning algorithm,

01:37:15.360 --> 01:37:24.760
a computer can detect signs of diabetic retinopathy,
a disease that can potentially blind more

01:37:24.760 --> 01:37:27.910
than 400 million people.

01:37:27.910 --> 01:37:33.619
Now, imagine that kind of insight spanning
the entire healthcare industry.

01:37:33.619 --> 01:37:41.099
So many forms of visual diagnosis may soon
be automated to help doctors and reduce overhead

01:37:41.099 --> 01:37:48.320
and errors and extending treatment to the
underserved population.

01:37:48.320 --> 01:37:54.590
Machines can also help to handle clerical
tasks such as scribing doctor visits, managing

01:37:54.590 --> 01:38:00.670
chronic diseases, leading to more reliable
and faster services.

01:38:00.670 --> 01:38:07.239
And this will culminate into full-scale smart
hospitals and homes with intelligent sensors

01:38:07.239 --> 01:38:15.130
to track hospital activity, keep patients
safe, ensure adherence to hygiene practices,

01:38:15.130 --> 01:38:17.460
and augment surgical protocols.

01:38:17.460 --> 01:38:25.110
I hope you're excited, just as I am, about
the opportunities that AI and machine learning

01:38:25.110 --> 01:38:27.060
can deliver.

01:38:27.060 --> 01:38:31.390
But this remains a field of high barriers.

01:38:31.390 --> 01:38:40.150
It requires rare expertise and resources few
companies can afford on their own.

01:38:40.150 --> 01:38:45.600
That's why cloud is the ideal platform for
AI.

01:38:45.600 --> 01:38:52.270
That's also why we're making huge investment
in cloud AI/ML that will emerge over the next

01:38:52.270 --> 01:38:59.130
year in the form of powerful, easy-to-use
tools that will give every cloud customer

01:38:59.130 --> 01:39:01.449
an on-ramp into this field.

01:39:01.449 --> 01:39:07.969
In other words, Google Cloud is democratizing
AI.

01:39:07.969 --> 01:39:17.960
This entails four broad steps: Democratizing
computing, democratizing algorithms, democratizing

01:39:17.960 --> 01:39:22.670
data, and democratizing talent and expertise.

01:39:22.670 --> 01:39:28.230
And let's talk about each -- what each of
these means.

01:39:28.230 --> 01:39:32.619
First and foremost, AI requires enormous computing.

01:39:32.619 --> 01:39:38.240
Today a deep-learning algorithm can easily
boast tens of millions of parameters and billions

01:39:38.240 --> 01:39:40.230
of connections.

01:39:40.230 --> 01:39:44.349
Training and using such models require computational
resource.

01:39:44.349 --> 01:39:50.130
Of course, that's exactly what the cloud was
designed to deliver.

01:39:50.130 --> 01:39:55.830
Last year, we introduced the beta version
of Cloud ML Engine.

01:39:55.830 --> 01:40:01.460
Today I'm here to announce its general availability.

01:40:01.460 --> 01:40:08.551
Cloud ML Engine is a platform that can harness
all the power, computing power, and deliver

01:40:08.551 --> 01:40:10.320
it to you transparently.

01:40:10.320 --> 01:40:18.639
Simply put, you develop the machine learning
models however you like using familiar tools

01:40:18.639 --> 01:40:22.409
like the TensorFlow library in your own environment.

01:40:22.409 --> 01:40:29.260
ML Engine allows you to focus on the creativity
of your solution and leave the infrastructure

01:40:29.260 --> 01:40:37.100
to us, then when it's time to train those
models upload them to the cloud where ML Engine

01:40:37.100 --> 01:40:41.969
can do it much faster and at a much larger
scale.

01:40:41.969 --> 01:40:50.590
Finally, deploy the result anywhere from your
own premises to a mobile device where it can

01:40:50.590 --> 01:40:55.270
put its training to use to solve real-world
problems.

01:40:55.270 --> 01:41:03.860
But even with the compute power in the world,
AI remains among the most complex field in

01:41:03.860 --> 01:41:05.540
computer science.

01:41:05.540 --> 01:41:12.239
And that's still a serious obstacle for many
enterprises and customers.

01:41:12.239 --> 01:41:19.160
For developers not quite ready to build their
own models, the easiest way to put AI to use

01:41:19.160 --> 01:41:26.020
today is through one of the APIs Google has
provided to deliver fully trained machine

01:41:26.020 --> 01:41:29.679
learning models to tackle common problems.

01:41:29.679 --> 01:41:38.030
These APIAPIs are like a switch that immediately
activate an intelligent component in any application,

01:41:38.030 --> 01:41:47.040
allowing it to understand speech and photos
or translate text or parse natural language.

01:41:47.040 --> 01:41:52.710
But there's a lot more to Google's depth and
breadth of AI technology.

01:41:52.710 --> 01:42:01.460
At Google, we have numerous research teams
housing an enormous amount of ongoing AI research

01:42:01.460 --> 01:42:05.520
spanning many areas of AI and machine learning.

01:42:05.520 --> 01:42:11.929
Our researchers are some of the most prolific
authors of scientific papers at top AI journals

01:42:11.929 --> 01:42:19.889
and conferences, and our teams are frequent
winners of best papers and AI competitions.

01:42:19.889 --> 01:42:26.139
And the results of this work are quickly turned
into products and services that we want to

01:42:26.139 --> 01:42:28.460
deliver to our customers.

01:42:28.460 --> 01:42:36.100
So I'm especially eager to announce some of
the latest products of that effort.

01:42:36.100 --> 01:42:46.390
The vision API has been under steady development
and features some significant new capabilities.

01:42:46.390 --> 01:42:54.330
First is an expansion of the API's metadata
to recognize millions of entities from Google's

01:42:54.330 --> 01:42:59.360
Knowledge Graph for images that are available
on the Web.

01:42:59.360 --> 01:43:05.110
We're now using the same metadata that powers
the entire Google Image Search.

01:43:05.110 --> 01:43:13.929
Second, an enhanced optical character recognition,
capable of retrieving text from images of

01:43:13.929 --> 01:43:21.410
text-heavy documents such as legal contracts
and other complex paperwork.

01:43:21.410 --> 01:43:26.840
But the world of pixels goes beyond just pictures.

01:43:26.840 --> 01:43:33.369
In fact, videos are among the most prevalent
form of Internet data.

01:43:33.369 --> 01:43:40.560
YouTube alone sees hundreds of hours of videos
uploaded every minute.

01:43:40.560 --> 01:43:45.429
Understanding the rich content of videos has
been a tremendous technology challenge for

01:43:45.429 --> 01:43:46.550
many years.

01:43:46.550 --> 01:43:54.360
In fact, many of us computer vision researchers,
we've often considered videos the dark matter

01:43:54.360 --> 01:43:56.409
of the digital universe.

01:43:56.409 --> 01:44:05.920
Today, I'm very excited to announce an entirely
new API powered by machine intelligence: The

01:44:05.920 --> 01:44:08.340
video intelligence API.

01:44:08.340 --> 01:44:15.190
I'd like to introduce my colleague, Sara Robinson,
to demonstrate this API in more details.

01:44:15.190 --> 01:44:16.190
Sara?

01:44:16.190 --> 01:44:19.000
Sara Robinson: Thank you, Fei Fei.

01:44:19.000 --> 01:44:21.719
[ Applause ]
Sara Robinson: Thank you.

01:44:21.719 --> 01:44:25.630
So the best way to experience the video API
is through a live demo and let's start by

01:44:25.630 --> 01:44:29.680
taking this video of a Super Bowl commercial
for Google Home and I'm going to play the

01:44:29.680 --> 01:44:30.680
first few seconds.

01:44:30.680 --> 01:44:35.470
We can see that it starts with a mountain
landscape and we see a house, a city street,

01:44:35.470 --> 01:44:38.710
then it goes to a dog in a garage.

01:44:38.710 --> 01:44:41.599
So lots of scene changes happening in this
video.

01:44:41.599 --> 01:44:44.761
And if we were to manually categorize what
was happening throughout it, we'd need to

01:44:44.761 --> 01:44:48.340
watch the entire thing and write down what
was happening in every scene.

01:44:48.340 --> 01:44:53.670
Luckily, the video API takes care of this
for us, which is a single REST API request.

01:44:53.670 --> 01:44:54.770
So it tells us two things.

01:44:54.770 --> 01:44:59.489
One, at a high level it tells us what is this
video about, and then it also tells us at

01:44:59.489 --> 01:45:03.360
a more granular level what labels it finds
in the video in each scene.

01:45:03.360 --> 01:45:08.160
So if we scroll down here, we can see it identifies
a dog and it can tell us exactly where in

01:45:08.160 --> 01:45:09.920
the video that dog appears.

01:45:09.920 --> 01:45:13.850
It also identifies at the end of the video,
there's a birthday cake.

01:45:13.850 --> 01:45:18.380
And if we scroll down a bit more, we can see
that not only does it know it's a dog, it

01:45:18.380 --> 01:45:20.610
knows what type of dog it is, that it's a
dachshund.

01:45:20.610 --> 01:45:24.909
And if we scroll down through the rest of
the labels, we can see that it also successfully

01:45:24.909 --> 01:45:28.910
identifies that mountain pass scene from the
beginning.

01:45:28.910 --> 01:45:32.930
So this is what the API can do with one video,
but you likely have more than one video that

01:45:32.930 --> 01:45:33.970
you want to analyze.

01:45:33.970 --> 01:45:36.901
So let's take a look at how a company might
use the video intelligence API.

01:45:36.901 --> 01:45:42.929
A media publisher could have hundreds of petabytes
of video data sitting in storage buckets and

01:45:42.929 --> 01:45:47.040
one common thing they might want to do is
create a highlight reel focused on a specific

01:45:47.040 --> 01:45:51.889
type of content or search their large library
for a specific entity.

01:45:51.889 --> 01:45:56.300
So let's see how we would use the video intelligence
API to search a large library of videos, given

01:45:56.300 --> 01:45:58.989
all this metadata that we get back from it.

01:45:58.989 --> 01:46:03.830
So we've got a lot of videos here and let's
say this media publisher has hours of sports

01:46:03.830 --> 01:46:07.020
video but they only want to find the content
relative to baseball.

01:46:07.020 --> 01:46:11.500
So let's go ahead and search our library here
for baseball videos.

01:46:11.500 --> 01:46:15.580
And we can see that not only does it show
us which videos have baseball, it tells us

01:46:15.580 --> 01:46:19.930
exactly when in those videos baseball appears.

01:46:19.930 --> 01:46:21.400
My favorite example is this one.

01:46:21.400 --> 01:46:25.680
We have this video which only has a tiny bit
about baseball but it's able to identify that

01:46:25.680 --> 01:46:29.820
clip for us, whereas if we were to manually
do this, we'd have to watch the whole video,

01:46:29.820 --> 01:46:31.090
looking for that specific scene.

01:46:31.090 --> 01:46:35.260
So if we click on this scene, we can see that
this is from last year's year-end search video

01:46:35.260 --> 01:46:38.239
when the Cubs won the World Series.

01:46:38.239 --> 01:46:39.950
So let's do one more search.

01:46:39.950 --> 01:46:42.239
I live on the East Coast where it's pretty
cold right now.

01:46:42.239 --> 01:46:45.420
I've heard there's been a ton of rain in SF
this past year.

01:46:45.420 --> 01:46:48.830
I think we can all agree it would be pretty
nice to be on a beach right now.

01:46:48.830 --> 01:46:53.429
And while machine learning can't take us there,
it can do the next best thing and find all

01:46:53.429 --> 01:46:56.530
the beach clips in our video library for us.

01:46:56.530 --> 01:47:03.360
So let's search for our beach videos, and
then we can click to all of our beach clips

01:47:03.360 --> 01:47:05.530
in the videos below.

01:47:05.530 --> 01:47:10.090
So as you saw through this demo, the video
intelligence API makes it easy to quickly

01:47:10.090 --> 01:47:14.960
and easily understand a large library of video
content, something that was almost impossible

01:47:14.960 --> 01:47:15.960
just a few months ago.

01:47:15.960 --> 01:47:21.679
Tasks that used to take hours now takes seconds
with the video intelligence API and I'm excited

01:47:21.679 --> 01:47:23.940
to make it available to all of you today.

01:47:23.940 --> 01:47:24.940
Thank you.

01:47:24.940 --> 01:47:28.800
[ Applause ]
Sara Robinson: And back to Fei Fei.

01:47:28.800 --> 01:47:31.219
Fei Fei Li: Thank you.

01:47:31.219 --> 01:47:32.690
Thank you, Sara.

01:47:32.690 --> 01:47:35.080
So I'm so excited to see this.

01:47:35.080 --> 01:47:40.750
As a computer vision researcher, I've seen
my field working on video understanding for

01:47:40.750 --> 01:47:46.620
decades, and now finally we're beginning to
shine light onto the dark matter of the digital

01:47:46.620 --> 01:47:53.800
universe and provide values to our customers
who can use it to harness the enormous amount

01:47:53.800 --> 01:47:57.159
of information embedded in these videos.

01:47:57.159 --> 01:47:59.659
Now, let's go on.

01:47:59.659 --> 01:48:05.030
Data is the next piece of the democratization
puzzle.

01:48:05.030 --> 01:48:10.490
Just as we learn through a lifetime of exposure
to the world to gain our human intelligence,

01:48:10.490 --> 01:48:16.730
AI requires a huge amount of data to develop
its own insight.

01:48:16.730 --> 01:48:22.300
But these data sets are among the steepest
barriers to overcome.

01:48:22.300 --> 01:48:29.090
I have a lot of personal experience of this,
having led the development of the ImageNet

01:48:29.090 --> 01:48:35.670
data set, which provides over 15 million labeled
images to the machine vision community.

01:48:35.670 --> 01:48:40.220
Many of you are now familiar with the history
after ImageNet.

01:48:40.220 --> 01:48:48.190
In 2012, it became one of the most important
enablers of the deep learning revolution.

01:48:48.190 --> 01:48:54.320
But to this day, it's still one of the most
used training data sets and benchmarks of

01:48:54.320 --> 01:48:56.710
deep learning algorithms.

01:48:56.710 --> 01:49:02.610
While the results of ImageNet have been incredible,
the long, difficult journey of putting it

01:49:02.610 --> 01:49:08.610
together was a tremendous testament of how
great the challenge still remains.

01:49:08.610 --> 01:49:16.540
What we need is a more scalable and effective
way to democratize data to more data scientists,

01:49:16.540 --> 01:49:22.820
machine learning developers, domain experts,
and eventually to our business.

01:49:22.820 --> 01:49:29.829
That's why I'm thrilled to make the next announcement,
Google Cloud's acquisition of Kaggle.

01:49:29.829 --> 01:49:39.000
[ Cheers and applause ]
Fei Fei Li: For years, led by cofounders Anthony

01:49:39.000 --> 01:49:46.139
Goldbloom and Ben Hamner, the Kaggle team
has been building an unprecedented community

01:49:46.139 --> 01:49:55.239
of over 850,000 data scientists, hosting contests,
and making new data sets publicly available.

01:49:55.239 --> 01:50:03.219
By merging with Google Cloud Platform, we're
giving this community direct access to the

01:50:03.219 --> 01:50:09.079
most advanced machine learning environment,
as well as providing a direct path to market

01:50:09.079 --> 01:50:10.909
their models.

01:50:10.909 --> 01:50:16.989
Working together with Kaggle, we're empowering
the largest concentration of machine learning

01:50:16.989 --> 01:50:18.590
talent in the world.

01:50:18.590 --> 01:50:25.369
In fact, Kaggle is already partnering with
Google Cloud and research to host the largest

01:50:25.369 --> 01:50:33.380
video understanding competition called the
YouTube-8 Million Video Understanding Challenge.

01:50:33.380 --> 01:50:43.010
So speaking of talent and expertise, we've
also been extremely committed to help our

01:50:43.010 --> 01:50:50.090
partners and customers to develop more machine
learning and AI expertise at the levels they

01:50:50.090 --> 01:50:51.090
need.

01:50:51.090 --> 01:50:56.469
At Google, we've always made significant investments
in research.

01:50:56.469 --> 01:51:04.610
Every year, Google gives large grants to over
250 academic research projects worldwide,

01:51:04.610 --> 01:51:10.469
supports dozens of Ph.D. students, and hosts
thousands of interns.

01:51:10.469 --> 01:51:18.420
Moreover, the Google Brain Residency Program
recognizes that expertise in AI will be an

01:51:18.420 --> 01:51:26.040
increasingly important resource in years ahead
and is taking steps to find, educate, and

01:51:26.040 --> 01:51:30.239
empower the future leaders of this field.

01:51:30.239 --> 01:51:35.910
At Google Cloud, in parallel with all these
efforts, we're equally committed to using

01:51:35.910 --> 01:51:41.340
our expertise to deliver real results right
now to our customers.

01:51:41.340 --> 01:51:48.139
The Advanced Solutions Lab allows customers
with more ambitious goals to partner directly

01:51:48.139 --> 01:51:52.540
with Google to solve complex AI problems.

01:51:52.540 --> 01:51:57.300
Let's take the insurance company USAA as a
recent example.

01:51:57.300 --> 01:52:03.179
Many of their engineers were well-versed in
data science, and some even had a background

01:52:03.179 --> 01:52:09.469
in machine learning, but they needed help
to build a true foundation of expertise.

01:52:09.469 --> 01:52:16.079
To do this, a team of USAA developers came
to Google's Advanced Solutions Lab where they

01:52:16.079 --> 01:52:21.590
learned directly from our own machine learning
engineers and experts.

01:52:21.590 --> 01:52:28.219
That team now is hard at work putting their
new skills to use, with additional teams being

01:52:28.219 --> 01:52:32.340
trained in the same way.

01:52:32.340 --> 01:52:40.889
So I think the most meaningful technologies
are the ones that transform a precious resource

01:52:40.889 --> 01:52:44.560
into something that can benefit everyone.

01:52:44.560 --> 01:52:52.140
The printing press helped literacy expand
beyond the privileged, made books so affordable

01:52:52.140 --> 01:52:57.640
that they could fill the shelves in homes
and libraries all over the world.

01:52:57.640 --> 01:53:05.929
The electric grid delivered power to entire
communities, turning heat and light from luxuries

01:53:05.929 --> 01:53:08.829
to staples of everyday life.

01:53:08.829 --> 01:53:15.949
The mass production of the Industrial Revolution
meant that artisanal objects that were once

01:53:15.949 --> 01:53:23.040
prohibitively expensive could now enrich the
lives of hundreds and millions of people.

01:53:23.040 --> 01:53:31.659
And of course the Internet has made everything
from newspaper to university courses so easy

01:53:31.659 --> 01:53:38.679
to share that they can reach a worldwide audience
overnight, often for free.

01:53:38.679 --> 01:53:45.870
What these examples have in common is the
transformation from exclusivity to ubiquity.

01:53:45.870 --> 01:53:54.889
I believe AI can deliver this transformation
at a scale we've never seen and imagined before,

01:53:54.889 --> 01:54:02.369
to help spread the luxuries of the privileged
few to the rest of us at a global scale.

01:54:02.369 --> 01:54:10.159
This is why I'm inviting everyone in this
audience to be part of this.

01:54:10.159 --> 01:54:18.960
We at Google Cloud are making the tools available,
but it's up to you to put them to use.

01:54:18.960 --> 01:54:27.650
And speaking of AI, it took a few years to
convince our next speaker of the power, the

01:54:27.650 --> 01:54:30.250
sweeping power, of AI.

01:54:30.250 --> 01:54:36.810
So if you're still on this journey, it's really
not a shame because you're in very good company.

01:54:36.810 --> 01:54:43.030
It's my honor and privilege to invite Mr.
Eric Schmidt.

01:54:43.030 --> 01:54:53.772
[ Applause ]
Eric Schmidt: Thank you very much, Fei Fei.

01:54:53.772 --> 01:54:54.840
Fei Fei Li: Good luck.

01:54:54.840 --> 01:54:55.840
Eric Schmidt: Thank you.

01:54:55.840 --> 01:55:00.619
Fei Fei said, "Leave the infrastructure to
us," and I don't think anyone could have said

01:55:00.619 --> 01:55:01.860
it better.

01:55:01.860 --> 01:55:06.389
Last year when I was here, I said, "We will
-- we will meet you where you are."

01:55:06.389 --> 01:55:11.949
What I've thought about this year is, "Just
get to the cloud now.

01:55:11.949 --> 01:55:13.430
Just -- just go there now.

01:55:13.430 --> 01:55:15.380
There's no time to waste anymore."

01:55:15.380 --> 01:55:21.079
There's lots of reasons and I think we've
talked about them this morning, but imagine

01:55:21.079 --> 01:55:25.679
a model that goes something like this: You're
sitting there and you are building something

01:55:25.679 --> 01:55:27.849
on a container on your laptop.

01:55:27.849 --> 01:55:33.250
You get it working and then you just release
it to the cloud and it scales infinitely.

01:55:33.250 --> 01:55:35.599
That's how easy this is now.

01:55:35.599 --> 01:55:39.530
That's how easy it is for you to do this as
an individual programmer.

01:55:39.530 --> 01:55:41.770
Now, does this matter?

01:55:41.770 --> 01:55:43.750
Well, it mattered in the last year.

01:55:43.750 --> 01:55:45.929
Let's think about Pokemon Go, right?

01:55:45.929 --> 01:55:54.429
So here's a fantastic product which had 50
times more demand in its first two hours than

01:55:54.429 --> 01:55:57.920
ever planned in their most optimistic forecasts.

01:55:57.920 --> 01:56:01.080
And I'm pleased to say that our system expanded
-- right?

01:56:01.080 --> 01:56:04.680
-- that the container architecture and the
use of Kubernetes actually could handle the

01:56:04.680 --> 01:56:05.680
load.

01:56:05.680 --> 01:56:10.960
And I'm quite convinced there was no other
way to handle such a global phenomenon.

01:56:10.960 --> 01:56:13.570
And you sit there and you go, "Well, I'm not
trying to be Pokemon Go."

01:56:13.570 --> 01:56:15.119
Well, if you could, you'd be pretty happy.

01:56:15.119 --> 01:56:17.370
[ Laughter ]
Eric Schmidt: Think about the sort of global

01:56:17.370 --> 01:56:18.440
success.

01:56:18.440 --> 01:56:22.450
So you might as well plan for global success
and infinite demand.

01:56:22.450 --> 01:56:27.310
And even if you don't hit it, your architecture
will be right and your costs will be lower.

01:56:27.310 --> 01:56:29.530
I'll give you another example.

01:56:29.530 --> 01:56:33.560
Start -- well, if it sounds like open source
-- right?

01:56:33.560 --> 01:56:39.570
-- using Kubernetes, Apache Beam, Spinnaker,
using GRPC, Redis, HBASE, you're going to

01:56:39.570 --> 01:56:40.570
have a blast.

01:56:40.570 --> 01:56:41.720
It's going to be a great week, right?

01:56:41.720 --> 01:56:45.650
Give you another example consistent with this.

01:56:45.650 --> 01:56:53.060
We just had the strongest IPO in a long time
in tech in Snap and Snapchat.

01:56:53.060 --> 01:56:56.969
As part of that, when you read the narrative,
there are two things that strike you.

01:56:56.969 --> 01:57:02.659
First was their incredibly fast software development
in their app, both on Android and iPhone,

01:57:02.659 --> 01:57:07.590
and in their community, with huge success,
new models, new models of interaction which

01:57:07.590 --> 01:57:10.540
you all know and many of you use.

01:57:10.540 --> 01:57:13.790
But the other confusion that's interesting
in their process was nobody could figure out

01:57:13.790 --> 01:57:16.220
how they could do this with so little capital.

01:57:16.220 --> 01:57:20.540
And the answer is because they used our infrastructure
-- right?

01:57:20.540 --> 01:57:23.760
-- in a deal that's a hugely successful deal
for both corporations.

01:57:23.760 --> 01:57:26.880
So you sit there and you go, "Well, why would
they do that?

01:57:26.880 --> 01:57:32.170
I mean, after all, had they spent $2 billion
in data centers, they'd have $2 billion worth

01:57:32.170 --> 01:57:33.170
of data centers."

01:57:33.170 --> 01:57:36.869
Yeah, but then they'd be putting 3 billion
and 4 billion and 5 billion and 6 billion

01:57:36.869 --> 01:57:42.770
in, whereas this way we can ride the scale
and the investment that we have done.

01:57:42.770 --> 01:57:44.750
So are you not planning to be like Snapchat?

01:57:44.750 --> 01:57:48.840
Well, I think everyone here who represents
a corporation would be -- would be happy to

01:57:48.840 --> 01:57:50.440
be as successful as they are.

01:57:50.440 --> 01:57:52.300
Makes sense, right?

01:57:52.300 --> 01:57:57.870
But the interesting insight is in the last
year, we've done two things since this conference

01:57:57.870 --> 01:58:00.429
that I'm particularly proud of.

01:58:00.429 --> 01:58:05.830
One Fei Fei talked about, which is the sort
of making machine learning real at a commercial-

01:58:05.830 --> 01:58:08.139
and enterprise-grade scale.

01:58:08.139 --> 01:58:10.040
You saw the demos, you understand the message.

01:58:10.040 --> 01:58:11.540
I'll talk about that in a minute.

01:58:11.540 --> 01:58:17.290
But the other thing that we did is we took
the Google network, which is vastly better

01:58:17.290 --> 01:58:22.139
than anything else you've ever seen, all that
dark fiber, all that special fiber, all that

01:58:22.139 --> 01:58:27.440
interconnect, and we made it directly accessible
to the programs that you're using.

01:58:27.440 --> 01:58:32.250
So using Snap as an example, not only can
they use our platform infrastructure, but

01:58:32.250 --> 01:58:37.280
they get access to their global customers
through our network by virtue of the way this

01:58:37.280 --> 01:58:38.280
works.

01:58:38.280 --> 01:58:39.280
That's a very big deal.

01:58:39.280 --> 01:58:41.780
So you say, "Well, I'm only trying to get
regional customers."

01:58:41.780 --> 01:58:42.909
Dream big.

01:58:42.909 --> 01:58:46.219
Think about having customers in pretty much
every country.

01:58:46.219 --> 01:58:48.820
You get the idea.

01:58:48.820 --> 01:58:54.020
So what I want you to do is I want you to
sort of understand that we can do both great

01:58:54.020 --> 01:59:00.650
cloud -- that is, great technology in the
cloud -- but we can also now work with you

01:59:00.650 --> 01:59:07.179
as a customer and a partner to build a business
that actually scales globally in which you

01:59:07.179 --> 01:59:11.590
make a lot of money, which is ultimately what
everybody cares about.

01:59:11.590 --> 01:59:16.500
So I want you to imagine that --
And the good news is -- and Fei Fei talked

01:59:16.500 --> 01:59:20.349
about, and the others have talked about it
as well -- we have this incredible leadership

01:59:20.349 --> 01:59:24.909
platform for the things that I think matter
for the future.

01:59:24.909 --> 01:59:30.650
But the truth is that most of our customers
are still struggling with the enormous challenge

01:59:30.650 --> 01:59:32.849
of their existing infrastructure.

01:59:32.849 --> 01:59:38.500
Most customers say, "That's a great speech,
but I'm still trying to deal with my non-X86

01:59:38.500 --> 01:59:43.920
apps, my AS/400 mainframe," all the kind of
stuff that they have.

01:59:43.920 --> 01:59:47.130
And many of you work in such companies or
run them.

01:59:47.130 --> 01:59:48.140
So what -- how do you do that?

01:59:48.140 --> 01:59:54.179
So let's imagine a sales call where I am your
salesperson visiting you and we say, "What

01:59:54.179 --> 01:59:55.179
are we going to do?"

01:59:55.179 --> 02:00:00.000
And what I'll tell you, the first thing to
do is take your x86 binaries that you have

02:00:00.000 --> 02:00:05.280
in some data center inside your company and
move them to the Google VM.

02:00:05.280 --> 02:00:06.450
And you go, "Huh."

02:00:06.450 --> 02:00:11.670
Well, turns out it's cheaper, and if it's
cheaper, that frees up some money to do the

02:00:11.670 --> 02:00:12.690
next step.

02:00:12.690 --> 02:00:15.310
So the first thing is the easiest one.

02:00:15.310 --> 02:00:21.530
Literally take the existing PCs and the existing
PC software -- that is, x86 software specifically

02:00:21.530 --> 02:00:22.960
-- and put it onto the Google VM.

02:00:22.960 --> 02:00:24.910
What do you do with the existing systems?

02:00:24.910 --> 02:00:26.450
You leave them running.

02:00:26.450 --> 02:00:27.450
Makes perfect sense.

02:00:27.450 --> 02:00:28.699
That's Phase 1.

02:00:28.699 --> 02:00:29.699
What do you do next?

02:00:29.699 --> 02:00:36.110
What you do is you take that system, now you've
got it working half in the cloud, half not,

02:00:36.110 --> 02:00:41.130
and you say, "I'm now carefully going to take
the data out of all these systems and put

02:00:41.130 --> 02:00:45.690
it in a modern, scalable, managed database,
either SQL or non-SQL."

02:00:45.690 --> 02:00:47.810
How do I do that?

02:00:47.810 --> 02:00:50.300
Well, we work with you to do that.

02:00:50.300 --> 02:00:55.920
And then of course over time, you build and
move that data to modern end-to-end services.

02:00:55.920 --> 02:01:01.119
I'll bet my -- the rest of my professional
career that the future of your business is

02:01:01.119 --> 02:01:06.070
big data and machine learning applied to the
business opportunities, customer challenges,

02:01:06.070 --> 02:01:07.320
and things before you.

02:01:07.320 --> 02:01:11.630
It's true whether it's the video API, an intelligent
service we just announced, or more traditional

02:01:11.630 --> 02:01:17.780
classification or the new deep learning approaches
that are being pioneered at Google and elsewhere.

02:01:17.780 --> 02:01:21.540
So what happens is, at that point you say,
"Okay, I managed to get the data over here.

02:01:21.540 --> 02:01:23.159
Now I don't know what to do."

02:01:23.159 --> 02:01:26.619
Well, Google has a lot of resources that we
can throw at this.

02:01:26.619 --> 02:01:28.050
We have customer engineers -- right?

02:01:28.050 --> 02:01:29.050
-- to help you.

02:01:29.050 --> 02:01:31.600
We've got an office of the CTO to help you
with the technology.

02:01:31.600 --> 02:01:36.219
We've got solutions architects who actually
do a whole business solution for you.

02:01:36.219 --> 02:01:39.640
We have an Advanced Solutions Lab that actually
showcases some of these things.

02:01:39.640 --> 02:01:44.790
We have professional services that you can
work with in the ways that you would imagine.

02:01:44.790 --> 02:01:49.290
We have people who can -- called strategic
customer engineers who help you build your

02:01:49.290 --> 02:01:50.290
cloud.

02:01:50.290 --> 02:01:53.770
And we finally have customer reliability engineering
to keep the thing running.

02:01:53.770 --> 02:01:54.770
Right?

02:01:54.770 --> 02:01:57.260
So not only is it --
It used to be we would say, "Well, we have

02:01:57.260 --> 02:02:01.350
this incredible technology derived from the
way Google operates," but we didn't have a

02:02:01.350 --> 02:02:03.460
full-service product offering.

02:02:03.460 --> 02:02:06.619
We didn't have all the service, we didn't
have all the people to help you.

02:02:06.619 --> 02:02:09.500
And you're sitting there going, "Well, do
I really believe you?"

02:02:09.500 --> 02:02:11.440
Well, we have the references now.

02:02:11.440 --> 02:02:14.300
They are among you and you've heard some of
them today.

02:02:14.300 --> 02:02:17.199
And what's interesting is that not only do
you have to do that, but you're going to need

02:02:17.199 --> 02:02:21.780
additional help, especially with the legacy
systems, so we have system integrator partners.

02:02:21.780 --> 02:02:27.320
So examples that I wrote down, Pythian, Agosto,
Groovenauts, SADA, global systems integrators

02:02:27.320 --> 02:02:29.730
like Accenture and PwC.

02:02:29.730 --> 02:02:31.890
So okay.

02:02:31.890 --> 02:02:33.590
That sounds like a pretty good pitch.

02:02:33.590 --> 02:02:38.920
The last time anybody looked at this, it takes
years to make this transition, so that was

02:02:38.920 --> 02:02:44.170
another problem that we faced a year ago and
we figured out how to solve that.

02:02:44.170 --> 02:02:49.260
We're working now to get these conversions
to occur in one month, two months, three months,

02:02:49.260 --> 02:02:50.280
four months.

02:02:50.280 --> 02:02:51.290
And why?

02:02:51.290 --> 02:02:53.360
Because time is everything.

02:02:53.360 --> 02:02:58.500
And you -- while you're doing this conversion,
to some degree some of your customer experience

02:02:58.500 --> 02:03:03.170
software, some of your value added is sort
of being held captive.

02:03:03.170 --> 02:03:07.889
So another example, we have Lush, which is
a cosmetic retailer, moved their point of

02:03:07.889 --> 02:03:10.150
sale in a month.

02:03:10.150 --> 02:03:13.510
Ocado rebuilt into the cloud in six hours.

02:03:13.510 --> 02:03:18.449
We used all of Evernote with three petabytes
of data in three months.

02:03:18.449 --> 02:03:23.510
That's how fast in the model that I'm describing
you can get there.

02:03:23.510 --> 02:03:27.020
So I think that there was a speed limit.

02:03:27.020 --> 02:03:31.409
That speed limit was defined by our ability
to work with the data that you had and the

02:03:31.409 --> 02:03:35.430
install base that you had and moving it into
this new model.

02:03:35.430 --> 02:03:36.949
The new model works.

02:03:36.949 --> 02:03:37.949
Trust me.

02:03:37.949 --> 02:03:41.250
If you're doing a new build, you're clearly
going to do that anyway because you're intelligent.

02:03:41.250 --> 02:03:43.310
The problem is you are stuck in between.

02:03:43.310 --> 02:03:44.630
You can't get to the new one.

02:03:44.630 --> 02:03:46.929
You are still stuck on the old one.

02:03:46.929 --> 02:03:48.070
So I don't know.

02:03:48.070 --> 02:03:49.219
What are some examples?

02:03:49.219 --> 02:03:53.809
If you have a data as an object, we now have
a software mechanism that will take the data

02:03:53.809 --> 02:03:58.599
and re- -- which we call by simple transfer
service -- which will basically just move

02:03:58.599 --> 02:04:00.860
it into our cloud structure; right?

02:04:00.860 --> 02:04:02.219
And just do it all over.

02:04:02.219 --> 02:04:04.330
That's how the data gets there so quickly.

02:04:04.330 --> 02:04:05.619
You built on containers.

02:04:05.619 --> 02:04:08.409
Again, we can support every model of containers.

02:04:08.409 --> 02:04:09.520
You're built on Hadoop.

02:04:09.520 --> 02:04:13.719
We love -- we love Hadoop in the sense that
it's very similar to the architectures it

02:04:13.719 --> 02:04:19.590
was based on, and it's easy to host it inside
of our model or use something even more extensive

02:04:19.590 --> 02:04:21.770
like BigQuery.

02:04:21.770 --> 02:04:23.400
You like virtual machines.

02:04:23.400 --> 02:04:25.389
As I said, just move them straight over.

02:04:25.389 --> 02:04:30.469
We've made huge strides in terms of engineering
investment and sales and customer service

02:04:30.469 --> 02:04:33.389
investment to scale that.

02:04:33.389 --> 02:04:36.099
So how do you do this?

02:04:36.099 --> 02:04:42.840
Well, first place, if you leave the infrastructure
to us, we put $30 billion -- and I know because

02:04:42.840 --> 02:04:47.670
I approved it, so it's real, right -- into
this platform.

02:04:47.670 --> 02:04:49.469
Please do not attempt to duplicate it.

02:04:49.469 --> 02:04:51.020
You have better uses of your money.

02:04:51.020 --> 02:04:55.659
I would much rather have you take the money
that you have and the talent that you have

02:04:55.659 --> 02:05:01.409
and build on top of this platform to provide
that rapid iteration and that rapid extension

02:05:01.409 --> 02:05:03.750
in the model that I'm describing.

02:05:03.750 --> 02:05:08.660
What's interesting is that we're continuing
to take this stuff and make it stronger.

02:05:08.660 --> 02:05:09.829
We just launched Spanner.

02:05:09.829 --> 02:05:14.100
And if you don't know what Spanner is, Spanner
is, in my view, a work of art in a computer

02:05:14.100 --> 02:05:15.790
science sense.

02:05:15.790 --> 02:05:18.969
It's a way of doing SQL, so it's a proper
database.

02:05:18.969 --> 02:05:25.161
But it's globally essentially replicated and
globally coherent at a scale of data that

02:05:25.161 --> 02:05:27.110
has never been seen before.

02:05:27.110 --> 02:05:30.040
And, by the way, we use it -- it's how Google
works.

02:05:30.040 --> 02:05:33.969
And, by the way, we released it a week ago
for the cloud.

02:05:33.969 --> 02:05:36.889
We have a container builder that does the
same thing.

02:05:36.889 --> 02:05:41.059
So if you think about it, your model is something
like this.

02:05:41.059 --> 02:05:44.219
BigQuery provides petabyte-scale analysis
on demand.

02:05:44.219 --> 02:05:50.620
Big Table provides millions of QPS, global
cloud storage for the actual millions of objects;

02:05:50.620 --> 02:05:51.620
right?

02:05:51.620 --> 02:05:53.070
Again, the prices are very, very low.

02:05:53.070 --> 02:06:01.780
In all of this platform that I'm describing,
"this get to the cloud now" model -- and most

02:06:01.780 --> 02:06:08.270
people, by the way, are still in the moving
their x86s into VMs, getting some of the data

02:06:08.270 --> 02:06:12.380
out of the VMs, putting them into proper databases;
right?

02:06:12.380 --> 02:06:16.409
So you're still in the sort of "getting there"
phase.

02:06:16.409 --> 02:06:21.460
Once you have the data, you have an opportunity
for real transformation.

02:06:21.460 --> 02:06:29.099
I think that big data is so powerful that
nation states will fight over how much data

02:06:29.099 --> 02:06:30.409
matters.

02:06:30.409 --> 02:06:36.099
That he who has the data that can do the analytics
and the algorithms that Fei Fei talked about

02:06:36.099 --> 02:06:41.059
at the scale that we are talking about will
provide huge nation state benefits in terms

02:06:41.059 --> 02:06:45.559
of global companies and benefits for their
citizens and so forth and so on.

02:06:45.559 --> 02:06:49.980
But this is all basically because once you
have the data, you have to do one more thing.

02:06:49.980 --> 02:06:56.219
You have to change from writing programs to
instead building programs that learn outcomes.

02:06:56.219 --> 02:07:02.170
And what machine learning really is, and AI
really is, is it's a change in the way you

02:07:02.170 --> 02:07:03.170
program.

02:07:03.170 --> 02:07:06.849
And over and over again -- and we've talked
about this at some length already as a company

02:07:06.849 --> 02:07:12.329
-- we've seen -- because of vision, right,
we have the possibility of drastically reducing

02:07:12.329 --> 02:07:15.909
the number of deaths in automobile accidents
and truck accidents.

02:07:15.909 --> 02:07:17.329
A big deal; right?

02:07:17.329 --> 02:07:18.679
A huge deal.

02:07:18.679 --> 02:07:23.750
And, by the way, accidents in cars are going
up right now in America because distracters

02:07:23.750 --> 02:07:28.460
appear -- drivers appear to be distracted
by the very technology that is going into

02:07:28.460 --> 02:07:29.520
the cars.

02:07:29.520 --> 02:07:30.780
So we've got to solve that problem.

02:07:30.780 --> 02:07:32.280
We've got to do it now.

02:07:32.280 --> 02:07:34.480
That is happening.

02:07:34.480 --> 02:07:40.310
In healthcare, in pathology, radiology, all
of the sort of patient-centered issues, we're

02:07:40.310 --> 02:07:42.610
not that different from each other.

02:07:42.610 --> 02:07:47.080
And the machine learning and collaborative
learning that's now possible really will produce

02:07:47.080 --> 02:07:50.170
amazing healthcare outcomes.

02:07:50.170 --> 02:07:53.809
And there's a possibility that the kind of
intelligence that is going to be buildable

02:07:53.809 --> 02:07:58.720
on these platforms will really surpass the
kind of insights that the average person in

02:07:58.720 --> 02:08:00.450
a business is going to have.

02:08:00.450 --> 02:08:05.910
We're seeing over and over again people are
using big data to do customer analysis, pattern-matching

02:08:05.910 --> 02:08:11.219
analysis, and customer targeting that really
does produce extra insight.

02:08:11.219 --> 02:08:15.719
And that extra insight is worth billions and
billions of dollars in their marketing programs

02:08:15.719 --> 02:08:17.220
and knowing it.

02:08:17.220 --> 02:08:22.159
So let me finish by saying that we're here
for real.

02:08:22.159 --> 02:08:23.610
You're here for real.

02:08:23.610 --> 02:08:28.550
This is an incredibly serious mission, something
I've wanted to do since I joined the company

02:08:28.550 --> 02:08:30.110
17 years ago.

02:08:30.110 --> 02:08:34.390
It's something that I know that the people
you've heard, whenever they started, care

02:08:34.390 --> 02:08:36.070
a great deal about it.

02:08:36.070 --> 02:08:42.540
The company has both the money, the means,
and the commitment to pull off a new platform

02:08:42.540 --> 02:08:46.780
of computation globally for everybody who
needs it.

02:08:46.780 --> 02:08:51.170
And something which allows you to be satisfied
that our principles, which are basically about

02:08:51.170 --> 02:08:57.440
openness and openness access, really will
allow you both the freedom of choice -- you

02:08:57.440 --> 02:09:01.130
won't be locked in -- but also the freedom
to innovate.

02:09:01.130 --> 02:09:06.530
When I look at our partners and I look at
the speed with which they can now use their

02:09:06.530 --> 02:09:12.599
scarce resources, which are always programmers,
to scale their business solutions, their enterprise

02:09:12.599 --> 02:09:16.250
solutions, their customer solutions, their
video solutions, and their marketing solutions,

02:09:16.250 --> 02:09:17.880
it just warms my heart.

02:09:17.880 --> 02:09:22.130
It's something we've looked at -- we've looked
forward to for decades.

02:09:22.130 --> 02:09:23.730
With that, thank you very much.

02:09:23.730 --> 02:09:24.730
And I'm going to bring you back to Diane.

02:09:24.730 --> 02:09:25.730
Thank you.

02:09:25.730 --> 02:09:33.930
[ Applause ]
Diane Greene: Thank you so much.

02:09:33.930 --> 02:09:39.230
Eric is always so wonderful to hear Eric's
brilliant insights and perspectives.

02:09:39.230 --> 02:09:45.610
He was one of the original enterprise people
way back when.

02:09:45.610 --> 02:09:52.179
So I want to just recap what you heard from
our customers, our partners.

02:09:52.179 --> 02:09:53.230
You heard from Disney.

02:09:53.230 --> 02:09:57.010
They're doing a full lift and shift to Google
Cloud.

02:09:57.010 --> 02:09:59.460
They're using -- they're going after machine
learning.

02:09:59.460 --> 02:10:03.949
I like to say bring the magic to the magic.

02:10:03.949 --> 02:10:09.910
Our SAP partnership so broad, you heard about
certifying HANA.

02:10:09.910 --> 02:10:16.150
You heard about integrating with G Suite,
the machine learning partnership, and also

02:10:16.150 --> 02:10:22.660
this very interesting partnership we're doing
for a data custodian to help our customers

02:10:22.660 --> 02:10:29.530
solve the difficult issues around compliance,
regulatory, security.

02:10:29.530 --> 02:10:36.929
And then you heard from our joint customer
Colgate, a 211-year-old company.

02:10:36.929 --> 02:10:40.550
You have to keep reinventing yourself to make
it that long.

02:10:40.550 --> 02:10:45.340
And they are transforming themselves today
with G Suite.

02:10:45.340 --> 02:10:50.559
And then Verizon moving 150,000 employees
to G Suite.

02:10:50.559 --> 02:10:55.810
Again, going after a cultural transformation
and dealing with some really difficult issues

02:10:55.810 --> 02:11:00.960
around moving to the cloud in their environment.

02:11:00.960 --> 02:11:06.960
Then we had Home Depot, very progressive,
forward-looking retailer looking to technology

02:11:06.960 --> 02:11:13.300
to get the advantage, looking to machine learning
to get the advantage, already taking advantage

02:11:13.300 --> 02:11:17.710
of Google scale to get them through things
like Black Friday.

02:11:17.710 --> 02:11:26.150
Then we had a very interesting look into one
of the world's largest banks, HSBC, about

02:11:26.150 --> 02:11:33.010
how they're going to use cloud -- Google Cloud
and the technology and the machine learning

02:11:33.010 --> 02:11:39.260
to really streamline their operations and
do things people didn't really realize was

02:11:39.260 --> 02:11:41.739
possible.

02:11:41.739 --> 02:11:46.470
And then we closed out with eBay.

02:11:46.470 --> 02:11:53.110
They definitely see Google Cloud as a technology
that gives them huge competitive advantage.

02:11:53.110 --> 02:11:59.360
Very interesting how easily they were able
to run their catalog on our cloud and work

02:11:59.360 --> 02:12:01.860
in a hybrid environment.

02:12:01.860 --> 02:12:07.159
And then, of course, we saw that wonderful
demo where the chat bot running on Google

02:12:07.159 --> 02:12:15.989
Cloud was talking to Google Home, giving answers
about the price of a camera in their catalog.

02:12:15.989 --> 02:12:19.409
Just terrific stuff.

02:12:19.409 --> 02:12:23.700
I want to also -- you know, where are all
the product announcements?

02:12:23.700 --> 02:12:26.450
All the product announcements, we have dozens
of them.

02:12:26.450 --> 02:12:28.619
They're tomorrow when you'll hear from our
leaders.

02:12:28.619 --> 02:12:30.070
You'll hear from Urs Holzle.

02:12:30.070 --> 02:12:32.870
You will hear from Brian Stevens.

02:12:32.870 --> 02:12:35.380
You will here from Prabhakar Raghavan about
G Suite.

02:12:35.380 --> 02:12:43.219
You will hear from Chet Kapoor, the CEO of
